uction of ornithine", "inputs": ["mol_025", "mol_003", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_027", "mol_026"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-370 K bulk collector; discharge is a nonequilibrium energy source", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic reducing gas/steam mixture exposed to electrical discharge, followed by aqueous collection and any necessary precursor hydrolysis; local discharge temperatures are not the bulk temperature."}, "mechanism": "Unresolved radical, carbonyl, nitrile, and nitrogen-incorporation chemistry produces a broad collected mixture containing ornithine or hydrolysable ornithine precursors.", "reasoning": "The supplied report attributes ornithine evidence to the volcanic-discharge archive analyzed by Johnson et al. (2008) and subsequent work in that lineage. This is an explicitly coarse-grained product-generation edge, not an identified elementary synthesis or selective preparation. Hydrogen closes a formal target-specific atom balance; it is not an experimentally assigned ornithine-specific coproduct. Ornithine yield, hydrolysis dependence, and accumulation remain uncertainties."}, {"id": "rxn_021", "name": "Candidate free-ornithine guanidination with cyanamide", "inputs": ["mol_027", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_024"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-350 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated ornithine and cyanamide; candidate mildly alkaline conditions; side-chain versus alpha-amino guanidination must be quantified."}, "mechanism": "The ornithine delta-amino group attacks cyanamide, installing the guanidino carbon and two additional nitrogen atoms.", "reasoning": "Longo et al. (2020) establish the chemical concept of ornithine-to-arginine conversion and identify cyanamide as a simple possible guanidinating reagent. Their quantitative peptide experiments do not establish this free-monomer cyanamide reaction in a naturalistic pool. Accordingly, environmental rate, regioselectivity, yield, and compatibility with discharge mixtures remain speculative."}, {"id": "rxn_022", "name": "Candidate alpha-linked ornithine dimer formation", "inputs": ["mol_027"], "outputs": ["mol_028", "mol_006"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "330-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": false, "other": "Concentrated dry-down phase of wet-dry cycling; no enzyme or modern coupling reagent; alpha-linkage selectivity is not assumed."}, "mechanism": "Intermolecular condensation of an ornithine carboxyl group with another ornithine alpha-amino group gives an alpha-linked dipeptide.", "reasoning": "Speculative productive edge included to represent the smallest explicit post-polymerization route, not as an efficient dimer synthesis. Frenkel-Pinter et al. (2019) show that ornithine undergoes competing lactamization and problematic oligomerization. Branched products and low conversion are expected risks; rxn_027 records the key lactam sink."}, {"id": "rxn_023", "name": "Candidate cyanamide guanidination of ornithine dipeptide side chains", "inputs": ["mol_028", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_029"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Rehydrated peptide-containing solution; candidate pH 8-10; sufficient cyanamide for both side chains; N-terminal modification is a competing reaction."}, "mechanism": "Guanidination of both ornithine delta-amino groups converts the model dipeptide to arginylarginine without requiring peptide-bond cleavage.", "reasoning": "Longo et al. (2020) demonstrate aqueous postsynthetic ornithine-to-arginine conversion on model polypeptides, but not this exact dipeptide with cyanamide under primitive conditions. The reagent subs