he endpoint.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.90,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.92,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.74,\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.80,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.86,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.82,\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_015\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_018\": 0.63,\n        \"rxn_019\": 0.55,\n        \"rxn_017\": 0.75\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.76,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.79,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.59,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.55,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.67,\n      \"feedback\": \"The intermediate sequence is coherent, but productive flux depends on inefficient early guanidination and unvalidated terminal thioamide processing. Patel et al. (2015) support the broader scaffold, not an established free-arginine endpoint through rxn_018 and rxn_019. Environmental feasibility remains conditional on specifying both the photochemical stages and the subsequent sulfur-replacement and amide-hydrolysis conditions.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_018\",\n        \"rxn_019\",\n        \"rxn_017\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Establish yields for the early-guanidinated branch separately from residual-cyanamide aldehyde trapping.\",\n        \"Validate terminal thioamide formation and processing independently before integrating them with upstream photochemistry.\",\n        \"Measure cyanamide consumption, sulfur speciation and product recovery during the transitions between reaction stages.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.90,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.92,\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.57,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.86,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.82,\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_015\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_018\": 0.63,\n        \"rxn_019\": 0.55,\n        \"rxn_017\": 0.75\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.73,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.77,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.59,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.52,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.65,\n      \"feedback\": \"This is a connected, balanced hypothesis with unresolved chemistry at sulfite-driven entry and terminal thioamide processing. Sulfite availability does not validate rxn_004, while earlier thioamide homologation does not validate rxn_018 or rxn_019. The network correctly retains downstream sulfide requirements, so sulfite substitution should not be interpreted as eliminating the need for a local sulfide source.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_018\",\n        \"rxn_019\",\n        \"rxn_017\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Validate sulfite reduction and terminal thioamide processing as separate modules using structurally verified substrates.\",\n        \"Quantify residual sulfite effects on cyanamide trapping and subsequent sulfide chemistry.\",\n        \"Provide a staged reagent-supply and recovery model, including the sulfide still required for homologation and terminal thioamide formation.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.90,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.92,\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.74,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.54,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.86,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.88,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.85,\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.82,\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_015\": 0.91,\n        \"rxn_018\": 0.63,\n        \"rxn_019\": 0.55,\n        \"rxn_017\": 0.75\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.70,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.74,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.57,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.49,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.62,\n      \"feedback\": \"The molecular sequence is internally consistent, but completion depends on inefficient early guanidination, unvalidated sulfite reduction of its product and unvalidated terminal thioamide processing. Explicitly labeling these transformations as hypotheses is appropriate literature alignment, but does not establish productive flux through them. The lower confidence reflects these chemical uncertainties, not pathway length or reuse of intermediates.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_018\",\n        \"rxn_019\",\n        \"rxn_017\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Require substrate-specific product identification and yields for rxn_008, rxn_018 and rxn_019 before treating this combination as a supported synthesis.\",\n        \"Benchmark early guanidination against aldehyde-stage trapping under comparable conditions.\",\n        \"Demonstrate that conditions selected for the proposed reactions preserve intermediates and permit subsequent quantified free-arginine release.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_020\": 0.66,\n        \"rxn_021\": 0.65\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.66,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.73,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.59,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.59,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.64,\n      \"feedback\": \"Discharge-derived ornithine followed by guanidination is a plausible strategy. The supplied volcanic-discharge literature supports ornithine occurrence, but rxn_020 is necessarily a coarse-grained mixture-production equation rather than an established selective transformation. Longo et al. (2020) support ornithine-to-arginine conversion conceptually, not quantitative free-ornithine conversion with cyanamide under the unspecified conditions here. Selectivity between the alpha- and delta-amino groups is a key target-identification issue.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_020\",\n        \"rxn_021\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Parameterize the discharge source with measured ornithine yields, collection conditions and any hydrolysis used during analytical workup.\",\n        \"Test free ornithine plus cyanamide over defined concentration, pH and temperature ranges, measuring cyanamide loss and competing guanidination products.\",\n        \"Distinguish arginine from positional guanidination isomers using structural analysis and authentic standards, not molecular mass alone.\",\n        \"Quantify rainout, concentration and recovery before downstream guanidination.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_020\": 0.66,\n        \"rxn_022\": 0.43,\n        \"rxn_023\": 0.52,\n        \"rxn_024\": 0.78\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.57,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.58,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.46,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.43,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.51,\n      \"feedback\": \"The principal bottleneck is selective formation of the alpha-linked ornithine dimer without specified activation or drying conditions. Frenkel-Pinter et al. (2019) support substantial competing ornithine lactamization, appropriately represented by rxn_027. Longo et al. (2020) support postsynthetic peptide guanidination as a concept, but not this cyanamide protocol or its selectivity. Peptide hydrolysis to free arginine is plausible; however, the Biochemical label alone specifies neither an abiotic setting nor suitable reaction conditions.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_020\",\n        \"rxn_022\",\n        \"rxn_023\",\n        \"rxn_024\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify and test an abiotic condensation protocol, quantifying alpha-linked dimer against delta-lactam, side-chain-linked products and other oligomers.\",\n        \"Demonstrate cyanamide-mediated conversion of both ornithine side chains while measuring N-terminal and other competing modifications.\",\n        \"Define drying, rehydration, guanidination and hydrolysis conditions with measured intermediate survival and recovery.\",\n        \"Quantify free arginine after peptide hydrolysis rather than treating peptide-bound guanidino-group formation as target achievement.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"best_pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n  \"overall_confidence\": 0.72,\n  \"network_feedback\": \"The network has sufficient molecule and reaction nodes, is connected, and uses molecule IDs consistently. Accepting the deterministic check as ground truth, all 27 reactions conserve atoms and charge with appropriate coefficients: there are no conservation violations. Factors of eight associated with S8 are ordinary formula bookkeeping, not implausibly large stoichiometries. The formal sulfur products nevertheless do not establish actual photochemical sulfur speciation or kinetics. Connected competing sinks rxn_025 and rxn_026 are chemically plausible, and rxn_027 reflects the literature-supported ornithine lactamization challenge; their rates relative to productive chemistry remain unspecified. Literature alignment is good in distinguishing the Patel et al. aminonitrile endpoint from quantified free arginine, treating sulfite and terminal thioamide substitutions as hypotheses, and avoiding unsupported glutamate-to-ornithine or direct Fischer-Tropsch arginine claims. Reaction-level citations and exact supporting conditions are still missing. P1 provides the strongest supported scaffold, but no pathway establishes a complete operating sequence yielding quantified free arginine. The major limitations are terminal hydrolysis conditions, substrate-specific validation of