free arginine is plausible; however, the Biochemical label alone specifies neither an abiotic setting nor suitable reaction conditions.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_020", "rxn_022", "rxn_023", "rxn_024"], "suggestions": ["Specify and test an abiotic condensation protocol, quantifying alpha-linked dimer against delta-lactam, side-chain-linked products and other oligomers.", "Demonstrate cyanamide-mediated conversion of both ornithine side chains while measuring N-terminal and other competing modifications.", "Define drying, rehydration, guanidination and hydrolysis conditions with measured intermediate survival and recovery.", "Quantify free arginine after peptide hydrolysis rather than treating peptide-bound guanidino-group formation as target achievement."]}], "best_pathway_id": "P1", "overall_confidence": 0.72, "network_feedback": "The network has sufficient molecule and reaction nodes, is connected, and uses molecule IDs consistently. Accepting the deterministic check as ground truth, all 27 reactions conserve atoms and charge with appropriate coefficients: there are no conservation violations. Factors of eight associated with S8 are ordinary formula bookkeeping, not implausibly large stoichiometries. The formal sulfur products nevertheless do not establish actual photochemical sulfur speciation or kinetics. Connected competing sinks rxn_025 and rxn_026 are chemically plausible, and rxn_027 reflects the literature-supported ornithine lactamization challenge; their rates relative to productive chemistry remain unspecified. Literature alignment is good in distinguishing the Patel et al. aminonitrile endpoint from quantified free arginine, treating sulfite and terminal thioamide substitutions as hypotheses, and avoiding unsupported glutamate-to-ornithine or direct Fischer-Tropsch arginine claims. Reaction-level citations and exact supporting conditions are still missing. P1 provides the strongest supported scaffold, but no pathway establishes a complete operating sequence yielding quantified free arginine. The major limitations are terminal hydrolysis conditions, substrate-specific validation of proposed substitutions, cyanamide guanidination selectivity, and productive ornithine oligomerization. Environmental consistency cannot be strongly established from Surface and Biochemical labels alone: temperature, pH, concentrations, irradiation, catalyst speciation, residence times, reagent delivery and transfer losses need explicit treatment. These are evidentiary and chemical-integration limitations, not penalties for particular feedstocks, shared intermediates, pathway counts or environmental ordering. Overall, this is a credible hypothesis network rather than an approved complete synthesis network; confidence remains below the required 0.90 threshold."}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 243671.47302627563, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-18T18:28:05.103588", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Arginine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\nThe following deep research report contains peer-reviewed literature on prebiotic synthesis\nof Arginine. Use it as evidence to inform your network design.\n\n=== DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of Arginine: Routes, Mechanisms, Geochemical Context, and Unresolved Problems\n\n## Executive assessment\n\nArginine is an unusually difficult amino acid to place in a prebiotic synthesis network. In the literature surveyed through October 2026, there is **no experimentally complete, geochemically self-consistent route that has taken only very simple early-Earth feedstocks all the way to an isolated, quantitatively characterized sample of free arginine under a single naturalistic set of conditions**. The evidence instead falls into several qualitatively different classes. The strongest modern de novo chemistry is the cyanosulfidic network of **Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy and Sutherland (2015, *Nature Chemistry*)**, which constructs the **\u03b1-aminonitrile precursor of arginine** from HCN/acetylene/NH\u2083/cyanamide/H\u2082S chemistry. The simplest late-stage route is **guanidination of ornithine**, for which aqueous ornithine-to-arginine conversion has strong chemical support, including complete conversion of ornithine residues in model polypeptides by **Longo et al. (2020, *PNAS*)**, although that experiment was not a naturalistic cyanamide-only prebiotic simulation.\n\nA third class is high-temperature heterogeneous catalysis. **Yoshino, Hayatsu and Anders (1971, *Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta*)** reported arginine among amino acids formed from CO + H\u2082 + NH\u2083 over Ni, alumina, or clay at 200\u2013700 \u00b0C, at amino-acid yields no higher than roughly 0.1%. The important caveat is that the companion isotopic/mass-spectrometric confirmation study by **Hayatsu, Studier and Anders (1971, *Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta*)** confirmed several basic amino acids\u2014including ornithine, lysine and histidine\u2014but **did not list arginine among the species confirmed from deuterated reactants**. The 1971 arginine assignment should therefore be regarded as a historically important but specifically **contested** direct synthesis claim.\n\nA fourth, metabolism-oriented framework is presently only a **partial precursor route**. **Kaur et al. (2024, *Chem*)** showed that H\u2082/NH\u2083 chemistry over Ni or meteoritic material can make glutamate and six other biological amino acids from \u03b1-ketoacids at 22 \u00b0C and pH 8. This is highly relevant to an extant-biochemistry-like origin of amino-acid metabolism, but the experiments do not continue glutamate through the chemically difficult ornithine/guanidino chemistry required to make arginine. Thus, there is still no demonstrated nonenzymatic counterpart of the complete modern biosynthetic route to arginine.\n\nThe evidence can be summarized as follows:\n\n| Framework | Immediate inputs to Arg branch | Furthest experimentally supported Arg-specific product | Conditions | Arg evidence | Main limitation |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Cyanosulfidic homologation** | HCN, acetylene, NH\u2083, cyanamide, H\u2082S, H\u2082O; Cu chemistry; UV | **Arg \u03b1-aminonitrile** | Aqueous, UV-driven, staged reagent additions; Cu(I)/Cu(II) can accelerate photoredox | Strong modern structural/mechanistic evidence for precursor | Free Arg itself not quantitatively isolated as endpoint; demanding staged geochemistry |\n| **Ornithine guanidination** | Ornithine + guanidinating reagent, potentially cyanamide | Arginine; particularly peptide-bound Arg | Aqueous guanidination | Complete Orn\u2192Arg conversion demonstrated on model polypeptides | Shifts problem to Orn supply; published experiment is not a direct naturalistic cyanamide simulation |\n| **Fischer\u2013Tropsch-type catalysis** | CO + H\u2082 + NH\u2083 | Free amino-acid mixture reported to contain Arg | 200\u2013700 \u00b0C, Ni/alumina/clay | Arg reported by amino-acid analyzer | Arg omitted from subsequent isotope/MS-confirmed list; very low selectivity/yield |\n| **Metabolism-like H\u2082/Ni chemistry** | \u03b1-ketoglutarate + NH\u2084\u207a + H\u2082, Ni/mineral | Glutamate, an upstream biochemical precursor | 22 \u00b0C, pH 8, typically 1\u20135 bar H\u2082 | Strong for precursor, none for Arg | Glu\u2192Orn\u2192guanidino chemistry absent |\n| **Historical nitrate/photochemical chemistry** | Reduced carbon/formaldehyde or carbohydrate + nitrate, light; oxide/metal catalysts in related experiments | Historical reports of complex amino-acid mixtures, including claims assigned to Arg | Sunlight, aqueous mixtures | Low-confidence historical evidence | Old analytical methods; mechanisms and product identity insufficiently validated by modern standards |\n\nThe overall conclusion is consequently **not that arginine is prebiotically inaccessible**, but that its origin is unusually dependent on how one solves two chemical problems: constructing a five-carbon \u03b1-amino-acid skeleton and installing the terminal **guanidinium** functionality. The cyanosulfidic route solves both in one connected synthetic network; the ornithine route separates them, making guanidination easy but leaving ornithine as the principal bottleneck.\n\n## Reaction networks and key intermediates\n\n**The cyanosulfidic route is the most explicit de novo reaction sequence presently available.** Patel et al. began the arginine branch with **HCN and acetylene**. Cu(I)-mediated cross-coupling gives **acrylonitrile**. Addition of ammonia to acrylonitrile gives **\u03b2-aminopropionitrile**. This C\u2083 aminonitrile is a crucial branch point because its amino group can be guanidinated by **cyanamide**, while its nitrile can be photoreduced and then repeatedly homologated to build the C\u2085 arginine skeleton. Patel et al. found direct cyanamide guanidination of \u03b2-aminopropionitrile to be relatively inefficient, leaving starting aminonitrile and cyanamide in the mixture; importantly, the remaining cyanamide becomes advantageous in the next step.\n\nThe sequence can be represented schematically as:\n\n**HCN + HC\u2261CH \u2192 CH\u2082=CH\u2013CN \u2192 NH\u2082CH\u2082CH\u2082CN \u2192 NH\u2082CH\u2082CH\u2082CHO \u2192 guanidinated C\u2083 aldehyde \u2192 homologated thioamide intermediates \u2192 C\u2085 cyanohydrin \u2192 arginine \u03b1-aminonitrile \u2192 [hydrolysis] \u2192 arginine.**\n\nIn greater mechanistic detail, H\u2082S/UV photoreduction converts \u03b2-aminopropionitrile into **\u03b2-aminopropionaldehyde**. When residual cyanamide is present, the aldehyde is rapidly trapped: cyanamide adds to the carbonyl and subsequent intramolecular chemistry generates a **guanidinated hemiaminal**. This aldehyde-stage guanidination was markedly more effective than trying to guanidinate the aminonitrile directly. HCN addition then gives a cyanohydrin; reaction with H\u2082S produces an **\u03b1-hydroxythioamide**; UV/H\u2082S deoxygenation produces a thioamide; further photoreduction, another HCN addition and finally NH\u2083 addition lead to compound 46, identified by Patel et al. as the **\u03b1-aminonitrile precursor of arginine**. A parallel unguanidinated branch cyclizes and gives the corresponding proline precursor.\n\nThat parallel chemistry is significant for network design. It means arginine is not generated by an isolated bespoke reaction: the Arg and Pro branches share **acrylonitrile \u2192 \u03b2-aminopropionitrile \u2192 \u03b2-aminopropionaldehyde** chemistry, with cyanamide determining whether a guanidinated open-chain branch is maintained. Patel et al. specifically inferred that when cyanamide and ammonia are present as acrylonitrile enters the network, the arginine and proline \u03b1-aminonitriles should be generated together.\n\nThe final step from the \u03b1-aminonitrile to free arginine is conceptually the hydrolytic endpoint of Strecker chemistry. The crucial evidentiary distinction is that **Patel et al.'s paper explicitly describes and characterizes compound 46 as the arginine \u03b1-aminonitrile precursor; it does not provide a headline quantitative isolated yield for free arginine itself**. For reaction-network purposes, compound 46 should therefore be represented as an experimentally supported node and **Arg itself as the expected hydrolysis product**, rather than treating the 2015 work as a direct quantified isolation of free Arg.\n\n**The ornithine route takes a different retrosynthetic cut.** Arginine differs from ornithine essentially by conversion of its terminal \u03b4-amino group into a guanidino group. Longo et al. emphasized that this transformation can be carried out chemically and that simple reagents such as **cyanamide, H\u2082N\u2013CN**, are capable of guanidinating amines. In formal mechanistic terms, the terminal amine acts as a nucleophile toward the electrophilic carbon of cyanamide and, after proton transfers/tautomerization, becomes the guanidino functionality of arginine. This avoids having to build the guanidinium group simultaneously with the carbon skeleton.\n\nThe key network is therefore simply:\n\n**ornithine + C\u2081N\u2082 guanidinating reagent \u2192 arginine.**\n\nThis becomes prebiotically interesting because ornithine itself has independent abiotic evidence. The reanalysis by **Johnson et al. (2008, *Science*)** of Miller's \u201cvolcanic\u201d spark-discharge apparatus found a broader amino-acid inventory than the classic experiment, and subsequent work by the same research lineage explicitly noted ornithine among products of the archived volcanic-discharge material. The apparatus incorporated a hot-water mist into the electrical discharge, providing a model of a steam-rich volcanic plume with lightning.\n\nThere is, however, an important distinction between **free-monomer** and **post-polymerization** versions of the ornithine route. Longo et al. experimentally showed that ornithine residues already present in model polypeptides can be converted to arginine residues in water. On model peptides they observed **complete Orn\u2192Arg conversion**, with much slower modification of the N-terminal amine and no detected modification of histidine side chains. On their 14-Orn primordial protein construct, reaction time was varied to isolate populations containing roughly **2\u20135, 5\u20137, and 8\u201310 converted residues per molecule**, confirmed by mass spectrometry.\n\nThis provides strong proof for the *chemical concept* of post-synthetic Orn\u2192Arg conversion, but the experiment should not be misrepresented: Longo et al. were testing an evolutionary protein scenario using a laboratory guanidination protocol. Their paper points out that **cyanamide is a plausible simple guanidinating reagent**, but their quantitative peptide experiment by itself is not an environmental simulation showing cyanamide-generated Arg in a pond, vent, or volcanic plume.\n\n**The heterogeneous CO/H\u2082/NH\u2083 route is chemically much less resolved.** Yoshino, Hayatsu and Anders reacted CO, H\u2082 and NH\u2083 at **200\u2013700 \u00b0C** over nickel, alumina or clay. The result was a complex organic mixture containing amino acids, hydrocarbons, purines and pyrimidines. Arginine, ornithine, lysine and histidine were among the amino acids assigned by an amino-acid analyzer, but there was no uniquely specified sequence of intermediates analogous to the Patel network. The chemistry was interpreted as a variant of Fischer\u2013Tropsch-type surface synthesis: CO is reduced and coupled on a heterogeneous surface, while nitrogen is introduced from ammonia into a broad family of products.\n\nThat mechanistic uncertainty matters. A network model should not encode this as a clean reaction of \u201cCO + H\u2082 + NH\u2083 \u2192 Arg.\u201d It is better represented as a **surface-catalyzed product-generating network with poorly defined C\u2013C growth, carbonyl/nitrile/amine intermediates, and very low Arg-specific selectivity**. The deuterium-labeling follow-up by Hayatsu, Studier and Anders demonstrated genuine catalytic synthesis for glycine, alanine, aspartate, glutamate, histidine, lysine, ornithine and tyrosine\u2014but did not include arginine in the confirmed list.\n\n**The metabolism-like route presently reaches only upstream precursors.** Kaur et al. showed that \u03b1-ketoglutarate can undergo H\u2082-driven reductive amination in the presence of ammonium and heterogeneous metal catalysts to give **glutamate**. Because glutamate/ornithine chemistry sits upstream of arginine in extant nitrogen metabolism, this supplies a plausible entry point for an evolutionarily continuous route, but no experimentally demonstrated nonenzymatic continuation to ornithine, citrulline, or arginine was established in that study.\n\nFor synthesis-network design, the most important intermediates can therefore be ranked as follows:\n\n| Intermediate | Why it matters |\n|---|---|\n| **Acrylonitrile** | Entry point into Patel's Pro/Arg C\u2083 branch |\n| **\u03b2-Aminopropionitrile** | Introduces the amino functionality; candidate for direct cyanamide guanidination |\n| **\u03b2-Aminopropionaldehyde** | Especially important because cyanamide traps it efficiently and diverts chemistry toward Arg |\n| **Cyanamide** | Supplies the guanidino carbon/nitrogens; potentially generated/stored independently |\n| **Guanidinated hemiaminal / aldehyde equivalents** | Preserve open-chain Arg branch during homologation |\n| **Thioamide intermediates** | Enable UV/H\u2082S deoxygenation/reduction during C-chain construction |\n| **Arginine \u03b1-aminonitrile** | Direct experimentally demonstrated Strecker-type precursor of Arg |\n| **Ornithine** | One-step carbon-skeleton precursor for guanidination route |\n| **Glutamate / \u03b1-ketoglutarate** | More remote precursor nodes in metabolism-like scenarios |\n\nThese intermediates divide the problem into two relatively independent modules: **C\u2085 skeleton construction** and **guanidinium installation**. That modularity is useful because no evidence requires that the two modules have the same environmental origin.\n\n## Conditions, mechanisms, catalysts, and quantitative evidence\n\nThe cyanosulfidic system is fundamentally **photochemical aqueous chemistry**, not classical hydrothermal high-temperature chemistry. Patel et al. used H\u2082S as a stoichiometric reductant and ultraviolet light to drive repeated nitrile/aldehyde/thioamide transformations; Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox cycling accelerates several of these reductions. In parts of the broader network H\u2082S can supply the reducing photochemistry without Cu(I), so copper is not universally mandatory, although Cu(I) is important in the HCN\u2013acetylene coupling that furnishes acrylonitrile.\n\nThe essential catalytic/reagent roles are chemically distinct:\n\n| Agent or surface | Role in Arg-related chemistry | Required? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Cu(I)** | HCN + acetylene cross-coupling to acrylonitrile; participates in cyanosulfidic photoredox chemistry | Important for entry chemistry |\n| **Cu(I)/Cu(II)** | Photoredox cycling accelerates UV-driven reduction | Accelerating rather than universally indispensable |\n| **H\u2082S / HS\u207b** | Stoichiometric photoreductant; also participates in thioamide formation | Central in demonstrated Patel route |\n| **UV light** | Generates reducing equivalents and drives photoreduction/deoxygenation | Central |\n| **Cyanamide** | Guanidinates the C\u2083 branch; especially effective after aldehyde generation | Central for Arg rather than Pro branch |\n| **NH\u2083** | Adds to acrylonitrile and later converts cyanohydrin chemistry into \u03b1-aminonitrile | Central |\n| **HCN/CN\u207b** | Carbon feedstock and homologating nucleophile | Central |\n| **Ni, alumina, clays** | Heterogeneous catalysts in 1971 CO/H\u2082/NH\u2083 synthesis | Central to that framework |\n| **Ni/SiO\u2082\u2013Al\u2082O\u2083, Pd/C, meteoritic metal** | Catalyze H\u2082-driven reductive amination of \u03b1-ketoacids | Central to precursor-only protometabolic framework |\n| **No added mineral catalyst** | Cyanamide/amine guanidination can be intrinsically chemical | Possible for the guanidination step |\n\nPatel's chemistry is not a one-pot reaction. The experimental logic repeatedly changes the solution composition: HCN, H\u2082S and ammonia are introduced at particular stages, photochemical reduction precedes later additions, and cyanamide's timing affects whether it survives long enough to intercept \u03b2-aminopropionaldehyde. The authors explicitly framed this as **systems chemistry involving spatially or temporally separated streams/pools**, rather than expecting every reagent to coexist from the outset.\n\nThat requirement is simultaneously a strength and a weakness. It avoids mutually incompatible reactions by spatial/temporal separation, but it raises the geochemical burden: a natural setting has to accomplish reagent storage, concentration, controlled release, irradiation and mixing in a chemically useful sequence. The paper's broader geochemical proposal invoked impact-generated cyanide chemistry, stable metal cyanide/ferrocyanide reservoirs, heating and hydration, together with sulfide and copper chemistry, to provide these inputs.\n\nQuantitative data for the **final Arg-specific branch** are less complete than one might infer from the route's prominence. Patel et al. describe direct \u03b2-aminopropionitrile guanidination as \u201crelatively inefficient,\u201d whereas trapping of photogenerated \u03b2-aminopropionaldehyde by residual cyanamide was sufficiently rapid that the guanidinated aldehyde-derived product dominated when irradiation followed immediately. The paper reports an explicit **>25% yield** for a related HCN\u2013acetylene oxidative coupling to cyanoacetylene, but that number belongs to another branch of the network and should **not** be quoted as an arginine yield. The main article does not supply a single overall isolated yield from simple feedstocks to free Arg.\n\nThe cyanosulfidic system was demonstrated with laboratory UV centered around **254 nm** and millimolar-scale sulfur photoreductants. This has motivated explicit early-Earth radiative-transfer and sulfur-budget studies rather than leaving environmental feasibility as a qualitative assumption. **Ranjan, Todd, Sutherland and Sasselov (2018, *Astrobiology*)** found that atmospherically supplied **H\u2082S-derived HS\u207b alone is generally difficult to accumulate to the concentrations desired for the Patel chemistry**, whereas SO\u2082-derived bisulfite/sulfite can reach more favorable concentrations in surface waters. They also calculated that surface UV remains available through most volcanic regimes, failing mainly under the very largest sulfur-loading events.\n\nThis creates a nuanced sulfur constraint: volcanic sulfur can simultaneously **supply reducing sulfur species and shield UV**. The modeling indicates a useful intermediate regime\u2014moderately elevated volcanism rather than arbitrarily sulfur-rich conditions. The same study suggests sulfite/bisulfite photochemistry could supplement or, for reactions where sulfur is needed only as an electron source rather than as an incorporated atom, substitute for HS\u207b. That is a concrete published modification to the original cyanosulfidic environmental picture rather than a rejection of the chemistry.\n\nUV itself is not the most serious global obstacle. **Ranjan and Sasselov (2017, *Astrobiology*)** modeled the 3.9 Ga surface UV environment and found that climatically reasonable CO\u2082 columns suppress the very shortest wavelengths while still permitting substantial prebiotic UV at longer wavelengths. Geography and surface albedo can alter photoreaction rates by more than an order of magnitude, so location matters, but a UV-driven surface route is physically possible.\n\nHCN supply is more conditional. **Tian and co-workers (2011, *Earth and Planetary Science Letters*)**, using atmospheric photochemical modeling, found that a methane-rich atmosphere could produce HCN deposition fluxes as high as roughly **10\u00b9\u2070 molecules cm\u207b\u00b2 s\u207b\u00b9** under favorable Archean compositions, whereas before biological methane production, abiotic HCN fluxes may have been only around **10\u2077 cm\u207b\u00b2 s\u207b\u00b9 or less**. Thus, a scenario requiring globally abundant dissolved HCN is much harder to justify than one that stores and concentrates intermittent HCN locally.\n\nA strong geochemical answer to that concentration problem was provided by **Toner and Catling (2019, *Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta*)**. Their experiments and aqueous modeling showed that **closed-basin alkaline lakes can precipitate sodium ferrocyanide over a broad range of plausible early-Earth conditions**. Subsequent thermal decomposition of ferrocyanide-bearing sediments can generate NaCN that redissolves into concentrated cyanide solutions. This provides a physically explicit concentration-and-storage mechanism for HCN-derived nitrogen/carbon rather than requiring a persistent concentrated atmospheric cyanide rain.\n\nLater experiments on ferrocyanide formation likewise found favorable formation at approximately **pH 8\u20139 and 20\u201330 \u00b0C**, with potentially significant concentrations attainable over years in appropriate lake models. This does not prove the complete Patel scenario occurred, but it substantially strengthens the plausibility of cyanide storage in shallow terrestrial settings.\n\nCyanamide has its own kinetic tradeoff. **Steinman, Lemmon and Calvin (1964, *PNAS*)** established cyanamide as a plausible reagent in chemical-evolution chemistry, demonstrating its ability to promote condensation chemistry in dilute water. Their experiments generally used **1\u201310 mM reactant concentrations at room temperature for about one day**, with many condensation products around **1\u20132%**. Later work has emphasized that cyanamide hydrolysis is thermodynamically favorable but sufficiently slow kinetically for cyanamide to persist and participate in other chemistry. This is advantageous for an Arg network: the reagent is reactive enough to guanidinate suitable intermediates yet is not instantly destroyed by water.\n\nThe heterogeneous high-temperature route is almost the opposite chemically. Yoshino et al. used **200\u2013700 \u00b0C** with Ni, alumina or clay. Total amino-acid yields were at most on the order of **0.1%**, and many other products formed simultaneously. Those conditions provide ample kinetic energy and catalytic C\u2013C chemistry but poor selectivity. The follow-up deuterium-labeling experiment was designed specifically to address contamination and demonstrated that several amino acids were genuinely synthesized from CO, ND\u2083 and D\u2082; its omission of arginine from the confirmed list is therefore a substantive reason not to treat the original Arg peak as secure.\n\nAnders, Hayatsu and Studier later argued in **1973, *Science*** that related surface-catalytic organic chemistry could operate in the early solar nebula around **360\u2013400 K at roughly 4\u201310 \u00d7 10\u207b\u2076 atm**, aided by magnetite and hydrated silicates. That argument supports Fischer\u2013Tropsch-like chemistry as a possible *source of extraterrestrial organics*, but it does not retroactively demonstrate arginine at those milder nebular conditions; the actual 1971 Arg claim came from the much hotter laboratory experiments.\n\nThe modern H\u2082/Ni work gives much better quantitative performance for simpler amino acids. Kaur et al. used water at **pH 8 and 22 \u00b0C**, typically 5 bar H\u2082, ammonium concentrations spanning approximately **6\u2013150 mM**, and Ni supported on SiO\u2082\u2013Al\u2082O\u2083, Pd/C, or ground Aletai meteorite. With isolated glyoxylate and six \u03b1-ketoacids, Ni/meteorite catalysts gave combined amino-acid plus hydroxy-acid yields of **6\u201384%**; Pd gave **52\u201388%**, usually with >5:1 selectivity toward amino acid over hydroxy acid. No reduction products were observed without catalyst.\n\nThose experiments are important precisely because they show where arginine remains exceptional. H\u2082/Ni chemistry readily performs the relatively simple **C=O \u2192 CH\u2013NH\u2082** transformation on \u03b1-ketoacids, including \u03b1-ketoglutarate \u2192 glutamate, but it does not automatically construct a distal amino group and then a guanidino group. The obstacle to Arg is therefore not generic amino-acid formation; it is **late-stage nitrogen functionalization of a comparatively elaborate side chain**.\n\n## Environmental settings and competing origin-of-life frameworks\n\nFor the **cyanosulfidic route**, the best-supported setting is a **UV-exposed, subaerial aqueous system with concentration and flow**: shallow lakes, pools, streams, impact basins, or hydrothermal/volcanic surface systems rather than a deep submarine vent. The chemistry requires light, access to concentrated cyanide species, sulfur photoreductants, and sequential mixing. Closed basins are particularly attractive because evaporation and mineral precipitation can concentrate otherwise dilute atmospheric products. Toner and Catling's ferrocyanide work gives this environmental class an experimentally modeled cyanide-concentration mechanism, while Ranjan et al. give it quantitative sulfur/UV boundary conditions.\n\nDeep-sea alkaline vents are therefore **not the natural setting for the demonstrated Patel Arg sequence**, even though H\u2082, sulfides, transition metals and pH gradients are abundant there. The decisive mismatch is photochemistry: the experimentally developed Arg branch depends on UV. A vent-derived reactant could in principle be transported to the surface, but that becomes a hybrid scenario rather than a purely submarine synthesis.\n\nFor **ornithine guanidination**, the environmental requirements are substantially looser at the conversion step itself. The chemistry can operate in water and does not intrinsically require a mineral surface. Its difficulty is upstream: one needs sufficient ornithine and a guanidinating reagent. The volcanic-discharge evidence makes a **volcanic plume/lightning \u2192 rainout/pool \u2192 guanidination** scenario conceivable. Johnson et al.'s reanalysis supports ornithine generation in steam-rich discharge chemistry, but it does not establish that the ornithine would accumulate together with cyanamide at concentrations that give high free-monomer Arg yields.\n\nThe post-polymerization version introduces a different environment: wet\u2013dry systems capable of making primitive peptides followed by aqueous guanidination. There is an important chemical tension here. **Frenkel-Pinter et al. (2019, *PNAS*)** showed that ornithine and related nonprotein cationic amino acids can **cyclize to lactams during prebiotic dry-down oligomerization**, whereas Arg, Lys and His gave more extensive and more protein-like oligomerization. Thus, an Orn-first scenario gains an easy guanidination target but suffers from Orn's tendency to undergo competing side-chain chemistry during peptide formation.\n\nFor the **H\u2082/Ni protometabolic framework**, mild hydrothermal or serpentinization-associated settings are much more natural. Kaur et al.'s reactions run near room temperature and neutral-to-mildly alkaline pH, use H\u2082 as reductant, and are promoted by Ni or Ni-bearing meteoritic material. H\u2082 and Ni are both relevant to water\u2013rock systems, and the authors explicitly positioned this chemistry as a geochemical analogue of ancient H\u2082-dependent metabolism. But because an Arg-specific continuation has not been demonstrated, this setting currently supports **pre-Arg carbon skeletons and amino acids**, not arginine itself.\n\nThe **Fischer\u2013Tropsch-like framework** is more compatible with hot mineral/gas interfaces, meteorite parent bodies, nebular grains, or localized high-temperature terrestrial environments than with a dilute prebiotic ocean. It has the conceptual advantage of very simple inputs\u2014CO, H\u2082 and NH\u2083\u2014and heterogeneous mineral catalysis. Its disadvantages are exactly what a synthesis-network designer would expect from high-temperature C\u2081 surface chemistry: broad product distributions, very poor compound-level selectivity, uncertain transfer from laboratory catalyst conditions to early-Earth settings, and questionable survival of complex amino acids in continuously hot zones.\n\nHistorical **nitrate/carbon photochemistry** deserves mention but much lower evidential weight. **Dhar and Mukherjee (1934, *Nature*)** exposed glycol or glucose plus nitrate to sunlight for **6\u20138 h** in the presence of TiO\u2082 and reported amino-acid formation by ninhydrin analysis; a representative setup contained 100 mL of N/2 nitrate solution, 5 g glucose and 1 g TiO\u2082 in an open Pyrex beaker. **Bahadur (1954, *Nature*)** subsequently explored amino-acid synthesis from paraformaldehyde and potassium nitrate under illumination, and related work in the same program investigated metal-assisted photochemical nitrogen fixation.\n\nThese experiments are historically relevant to an oxidized-nitrogen/formaldehyde framework, but they should not be put on the same footing as modern LC-MS/NMR-supported synthesis. Product identification relied on the analytical technology of the period\u2014color reactions and chromatography\u2014and the systems used large concentrations of already-complex carbon substrates in some cases. For arginine specifically, the historical product assignments have not acquired the kind of isotopic and high-resolution mass-spectrometric confirmation now expected. I therefore classify the nitrate/formaldehyde/sunlight literature as **hypothesis-generating evidence, not a robust modern Arg synthesis route**.\n\nThe environmental comparison is consequently:\n\n| Candidate environment | Arg route it best supports | Positive evidence | Principal incompatibility |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Evaporative/closed-basin surface lake** | Cyanosulfidic | Cyanide/ferrocyanide concentration; UV; wet\u2013dry concentration | Need coordinated cyanamide, sulfur, acetylene, Cu, reagent sequencing |\n| **Volcanic/impact surface hydrothermal pools** | Cyanosulfidic / Orn route | UV, sulfur gases, metal-rich rock, episodic concentration and flow | Strong sulfur can attenuate UV; HCN supply variable |\n| **Volcanic lightning plume and rainout** | Ornithine precursor | Ornithine demonstrated in volcanic-discharge material | No complete Orn + cyanamide \u2192 environmental Arg experiment |\n| **Serpentinizing/hydrothermal H\u2082 system** | Metabolism-like precursor route | H\u2082/Ni reductive amination under mild conditions | No demonstrated Glu\u2192Orn\u2192Arg continuation; UV-dependent Patel route incompatible at depth |\n| **Hot mineral/gas interface or parent body** | Fischer\u2013Tropsch type | Direct simple feedstocks; mineral catalysis | Low yields/selectivity; Arg assignment specifically uncertain |\n| **Sunlit nitrate/formaldehyde pool** | Historical photochemical claims | Early laboratory amino-acid formation | Weak product identification, concentrated/reactive substrates, poor mechanistic resolution |\n\nNo environment wins unambiguously. Surface cyanosulfidic systems have the **best-developed explicit Arg-forming chemical sequence**; H\u2082/mineral systems have the **best congruence with ancient biochemical reductants and metal catalysis** but lack the Arg-specific transformations; Orn guanidination is **chemically shortest** but depends on upstream supply.\n\n## Evidence strength and major published criticisms\n\n**Cyanosulfidic route \u2014 strong chemistry, contested geochemical integration.** The central strength is that Patel et al. did not merely infer arginine from a complex mixture: they worked through identifiable intermediates and explicitly obtained the Arg \u03b1-aminonitrile precursor. The route also shares chemistry with precursors of several other amino acids, nucleotides and lipids, which is attractive for systems-level parsimony. The weakness is that chemical success is distributed over **multiple controlled stages**, each of which has environmental requirements.\n\nThe first published geochemical concern is **reagent abundance**. Ranjan et al.'s sulfur modeling found that the atmospheric H\u2082S pathway does not straightforwardly supply the desired HS\u207b concentrations to generic surface waters; favorable sulfur photochemistry is easier to obtain from SO\u2082-derived anions or more specialized local sources. That is a genuine constraint on simply mapping the laboratory recipe onto a lake.\n\nThe second is **HCN accumulation**. Atmospheric modeling shows that high HCN production depends strongly on atmospheric reduction state, especially methane abundance. Low prebiotic abiotic fluxes motivate the need for capture, mineral storage and evaporation rather than a globally cyanide-rich ocean. Ferrocyanide-lake experiments provide a plausible solution but introduce an additional geochemical sequence\u2014capture, precipitation, burial/heating and redissolution\u2014that must occur at useful rates.\n\nThe third is **UV\u2013sulfur compatibility**. Too little sulfur reduces photochemical reducing power; too much volcanic H\u2082S/SO\u2082 and sulfur aerosol suppresses the same UV needed to run the chemistry. Quantitative radiative-transfer work nevertheless finds a nonempty parameter space, especially during moderately elevated rather than extreme volcanism. Thus this is a constraint, not a fatal contradiction.\n\nThe fourth is **overall yield accounting**. The literature provides yields for many individual transformations, but an overall environmental yield of free Arg from HCN/acetylene through every stage has not been established. For network comparison, multiplying the best isolated step yields would itself be misleading because sequential losses, side reactions, dilution and reagent destruction in a natural setting have not been jointly measured. Patel's final experimentally secure claim should remain \u201carginine \u03b1-aminonitrile precursor,\u201d not \u201chigh-yield arginine.\u201d\n\n**Ornithine guanidination \u2014 strong late-stage reaction, weak complete provenance.** Its strongest point is almost the inverse: once an appropriate Orn-containing substrate exists, Arg is chemically close. Longo et al. achieved complete conversion on model polypeptides and demonstrated by mass spectrometry that partial conversion can be continuously tuned. This is unusually good evidence for an origin-of-proteins hypothesis in which a simpler basic residue is chemically upgraded after polymerization.\n\nIts major criticism is that it **moves rather than solves the carbon-skeleton problem**. A useful free-Arg synthesis requires an adequate source of ornithine. Volcanic spark chemistry provides experimental support for Orn formation, but no experiment presently connects volcanic Orn formation, environmental accumulation, cyanamide availability and quantitative free-Arg production into one continuous sequence.\n\nA second criticism is the peptide context. Ornithine has an additional nucleophilic side-chain amine and can form lactams or branched products under condensation conditions. Frenkel-Pinter et al.'s dry-down experiments directly demonstrate this competing chemistry. That makes the ordering of events important: **Orn synthesis \u2192 peptide incorporation \u2192 guanidination** may not be automatically more efficient than **Orn \u2192 Arg \u2192 Arg-containing peptide**, and neither sequence has yet been established as environmentally dominant.\n\nA third is guanidinating-reagent provenance. Cyanamide is a highly useful prebiotic reagent, and both classic chemical-evolution work and modern cyanosulfidic chemistry provide routes and roles for it. But cyanamide is not indefinitely stable in water and also participates in numerous competing reactions, including phosphorylation/condensation chemistry. Its concentration, lifetime and partitioning in a realistic mixed prebiotic network remain important kinetic variables.\n\n**Fischer\u2013Tropsch-type Arg \u2014 direct claim, low confidence at the compound-specific level.** The attraction is obvious: CO, H\u2082 and NH\u2083 are far simpler starting materials than \u03b2-aminopropionaldehyde or ornithine, and mineral surface catalysis eliminates the need for a sophisticated photochemical sequence. The problem is analytical. The initial amino-acid analyzer experiment acknowledged some contamination while considering Arg \u201cdefinitely synthesized\u201d; the subsequent deuterated-feedstock mass-spectrometric experiment, specifically designed to rule out contamination, confirmed many other amino acids but not Arg. The correct evidence grade for Arg itself is therefore substantially lower than for ornithine or glutamate from the same broad catalytic family.\n\nHigh temperature is another problem. The 200\u2013700 \u00b0C synthesis zone is not a setting in which newly generated Arg necessarily accumulates for long periods in aqueous solution. One must invoke rapid removal, cooling or downstream collection. The later solar-nebula model operated at lower nominal temperatures, but that model was for broad organic synthesis and does not constitute a replicated lower-temperature Arg experiment.\n\n**Metabolism-like H\u2082/Ni chemistry \u2014 environmentally coherent, Arg branch missing.** This is arguably the strongest counterpoint to cyanide-centric chemistry because it uses H\u2082 as biological metabolism does, works at pH 8 and 22 \u00b0C, tolerates modest ammonium concentrations, and is catalyzed by Ni or actual meteoritic material. Its yields for simpler amino acids are orders of magnitude more attractive than the old FTT mixtures.\n\nBut this framework currently has **no Arg synthesis demonstration**. It solves reductive amination, not guanidination or distal side-chain amination. In the one-pot experiments, even some intermediates readily generated in isolated reactions were suppressed or undetectable when the network became more complex; for example, Kaur et al. report that glutamate was not detectable in one of the full one-pot network experiments despite its accessibility from \u03b1-ketoglutarate separately. This underscores a general lesson for Arg research: compatibility of individually successful reactions cannot be assumed in a network.\n\n**Historical nitrate photochemistry \u2014 intriguing but not analytically decisive.** The early work deserves preservation in a comprehensive survey because it shows that strongly oxidized nitrogen feedstocks need not be excluded a priori. Dhar and Mukherjee demonstrated sunlight/TiO\u2082 amino-acid-forming chemistry from nitrate plus reduced carbon decades before modern origin-of-life chemistry. Bahadur then explored formaldehyde/nitrate chemistry. However, neither the analytical selectivity nor mechanistic resolution approaches current standards, and the high starting concentrations and use of already reduced/complex organic substrates make direct extrapolation to Hadean environments uncertain.\n\n## Open questions and priorities for synthesis-network design\n\nThe largest unresolved question is **whether free arginine was ever an abundant prebiotic monomer at all**. Longo et al. explicitly motivate their Orn-first model from the observation that Arg is conspicuously difficult to obtain in simple abiotic synthesis compared with simpler amino acids. It is entirely possible that Arg became significant only after chemical networks had already reached ornithine, peptides, or some degree of protometabolic organization. That interpretation is compatible with both the cyanosulfidic precursor route and the postsynthetic Orn\u2192Arg route; the evidence does not yet discriminate between them.\n\nA central experimental gap is a **full mass balance for the Patel Arg branch**. The decisive experiment would begin from acrylonitrile\u2014or, ideally, HCN plus acetylene\u2014run the complete sequence under one environmentally motivated cycling protocol, hydrolyze the \u03b1-aminonitrile, and quantify Arg by isotope-dilution LC-MS or an equivalent modern method. The study should separately quantify proline, cyclic side products, cyanamide loss, sulfur products and unreacted nitriles. Patel et al. established the reaction logic, but not that end-to-end environmental mass balance.\n\nA second major gap is the **ornithine-to-arginine reaction under genuinely primitive guanidination conditions**. Modern work establishes that chemical guanidination is possible and that cyanamide is a simple candidate reagent, but an especially valuable experiment would measure free Orn + cyanamide over a matrix of pH, ionic strength, Mg\u00b2\u207a/Ca\u00b2\u207a/Fe\u00b2\u207a, phosphate, sulfide/sulfite, carbonate, wet\u2013dry cycles and realistic cyanamide concentrations. The key outputs should be pseudo-first-order rate constants, activation parameters, Arg yield, cyanamide hydrolysis, dicyandiamide formation and side-chain selectivity. Existing peptide guanidination data show the transformation is chemically favorable enough to deserve this direct environmental test.\n\nA third gap is **ornithine synthesis itself**. Volcanic discharge experiments establish that Orn can emerge abiotically, but yields and concentrating mechanisms relevant to downstream guanidination remain underdeveloped compared with glycine or alanine. A useful network analysis should therefore not enter \u201cornithine\u201d as a cost-free feedstock. It should be assigned an upstream formation penalty, competing lactamization chemistry, and an environmental concentration requirement.\n\nA fourth question is whether **a nonenzymatic metabolism-like bridge from glutamate to ornithine can be discovered**. H\u2082/Ni chemistry now provides a credible route to glutamate from \u03b1-ketoglutarate, but modern arginine biosynthesis requires substantially more elaborate nitrogen and energy-transfer chemistry downstream. Demonstrating even a plausible geochemical Glu\u2192Orn transformation under the same mild H\u2082/NH\u2083/mineral conditions would materially strengthen the metabolism-first framework. At present that bridge is missing, so claims of a complete \u201cprebiotic arginine biosynthesis\u201d from CO\u2082/H\u2082/NH\u2083 via an rTCA-like network would run ahead of the evidence.\n\nA fifth question concerns **environmental co-location versus environmental succession**. The most realistic solution may not be one setting. HCN might be produced atmospherically, captured as ferrocyanide in alkaline lakes, thermally processed during volcanism or impacts, released into UV-irradiated pools, and only later encounter sulfur/copper chemistry. Ornithine might originate in lightning-rich plumes and meet cyanamide downstream. Such linked environments are not intrinsically implausible, but every transfer introduces dilution and destruction. Quantitative transport-and-reaction models are needed before multi-setting scenarios can be ranked against single-setting H\u2082/mineral chemistry.\n\nA sixth issue is **competition for cyanamide**. Cyanamide is useful in nucleotide chemistry, phosphorylation, peptide condensation and Arg synthesis. Steinman et al. already demonstrated its participation in multiple condensation reactions, and modern studies show that carbonyl compounds such as glyoxylate and pyruvate can strongly modify its kinetics. In a realistic mixed system, cyanamide consumption cannot be allocated independently to every proposed pathway. Arg network simulations should explicitly include competing sinks rather than treating cyanamide concentration as fixed.\n\nThe seventh problem is **kinetic rather than merely thermodynamic compatibility**. Much prebiotic-network discussion asks whether individual transformations are energetically possible; the Arg literature illustrates why this is insufficient. In Patel chemistry, timing controls whether cyanamide reacts inefficiently with \u03b2-aminopropionitrile or rapidly traps \u03b2-aminopropionaldehyde. In the Ni/H\u2082 system, isolated \u03b1-ketoacid reductive amination can be high-yielding while some products disappear in the larger one-pot network. The relevant design variable is therefore not merely \u0394G but the ordering and relative rates of productive reactions, hydrolysis, cyclization, photolysis and reagent depletion.\n\nOn current evidence, a reasonable ranking for **experimental support specifically for producing an Arg-relevant chemical node** is:\n\n**Cyanosulfidic \u03b1-aminonitrile route > ornithine guanidination as a late-stage chemical transformation > FTT direct-Arg claim > historical nitrate/formaldehyde Arg claims**, while **H\u2082/Ni protometabolism has strong evidence only for upstream precursors rather than Arg itself**. That ordering is an evidence ranking, not a claim about which origin-of-life framework actually occurred.\n\nFor network construction, I would therefore encode at least three competing Arg modules rather than forcing a single canonical pathway:\n\n**Module A \u2014 cyanosulfidic:**  \nHCN + C\u2082H\u2082 \u2192 acrylonitrile \u2192 \u03b2-aminopropionitrile \u2192 \u03b2-aminopropionaldehyde \u2192 cyanamide-trapped guanidinated intermediates \u2192 thioamide chemistry \u2192 Arg \u03b1-aminonitrile \u2192 Arg.\n\n**Module B \u2014 Orn-first:**  \nabiotic C\u2085 amino-acid chemistry \u2192 ornithine \u2192 cyanamide/other primitive guanidination \u2192 Arg, either before or after incorporation into oligomers.\n\n**Module C \u2014 protometabolic:**  \nCO\u2082-derived carbon network \u2192 \u03b1-ketoglutarate \u2192 glutamate \u2192 **unknown/prebiotically unresolved steps** \u2192 ornithine/guanidino intermediates \u2192 Arg.\n\nA fourth low-confidence module can retain **CO + H\u2082 + NH\u2083 heterogeneous surface synthesis \u2192 complex amino-acid mixture \u2192 putative Arg** so that the historical FTT evidence is not discarded, but its Arg edge should carry a substantially lower confidence score because the isotope-confirmation study did not reproduce the Arg identification.\n\n## Key primary literature\n\n| Study | Journal | Relevance to arginine |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Patel, B. H.; Percivalle, C.; Ritson, D. J.; Duffy, C. D.; Sutherland, J. D. (2015)**, \u201cCommon origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a cyanosulfidic protometabolism\u201d | *Nature Chemistry* 7, 301\u2013307 | Principal modern de novo route; constructs the \u03b1-aminonitrile precursor of Arg from acrylonitrile/HCN/cyanamide/H\u2082S chemistry. |\n| **Longo, L. M.; Despotovi\u0107, D.; Weil-Ktorza, O.; Walker, M. J.; Jab\u0142o\u0144ska, J.; Fridmann-Sirkis, Y.; Varani, G.; Metanis, N.; Tawfik, D. S. (2020)**, \u201cPrimordial emergence of a nucleic acid-binding protein via phase separation and statistical ornithine-to-arginine conversion\u201d | *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences USA* 117, 15731\u201315739 | Demonstrates aqueous Orn\u2192Arg conversion on model polypeptides; complete conversion on test peptides; establishes postsynthetic Arg scenario. |\n| **Johnson, A. P.; Cleaves, H. J.; Dworkin, J. P.; Glavin, D. P.; Lazcano, A.; Bada, J. L. (2008)**, \u201cThe Miller volcanic spark discharge experiment\u201d | *Science* 322, 404 | Modern analysis of archived volcanic-discharge experiment supporting abiotic production of a broader amino-acid set including Orn-related evidence. |\n| **Yoshino, D.; Hayatsu, K.; Anders, E. (1971)**, \u201cOrigin of organic matter in early solar system\u2014III. Amino acids: Catalytic synthesis\u201d | *Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta* 35, 927\u2013938 | Reports Arg from CO/H\u2082/NH\u2083 at 200\u2013700 \u00b0C over Ni, alumina or clay; yields \u2264~0.1%, complex mixture. |\n| **Hayatsu, R.; Studier, M. H.; Anders, E. (1971)**, \u201cOrigin of organic matter in early solar system\u2014IV. Amino acids: Confirmation of catalytic synthesis by mass spectrometry\u201d | *Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta* 35, 939\u2013951 | Deuterium-label confirmation of catalytic amino-acid synthesis; importantly does **not** list Arg among confirmed compounds. |\n| **Kaur, H.; Rauscher, S. A.; Werner, E.; Song, Y.; Yi, J.; Kaz\u00f6ne, W.; Martin, W. F.; T\u00fcys\u00fcz, H.; Moran, J. (2024)**, \u201cA prebiotic Krebs cycle analog generates amino acids with H\u2082 and NH\u2083 over nickel\u201d | *Chem* 10, 1528\u20131540 | Mild H\u2082/Ni reductive amination produces glutamate and other amino acids; strong upstream evidence but no Arg. |\n| **Frenkel-Pinter, M. et al. (2019)**, \u201cSelective incorporation of proteinaceous over nonproteinaceous cationic amino acids in model prebiotic oligomerization reactions\u201d | *PNAS* 116, 16338\u201316346 | Shows competing Orn lactamization and different oligomerization behavior of Arg/Orn; important critique of Orn-first peptide scenarios. |\n| **Ranjan, S.; Todd, Z. R.; Sutherland, J. D.; Sasselov, D. D. (2018)**, \u201cSulfidic Anion Concentrations on Early Earth for Surficial Origins-of-Life Chemistry\u201d | *Astrobiology* | Quantitative test of sulfur availability and UV compatibility for cyanosulfidic chemistry; atmospheric H\u2082S source is constrained, SO\u2082-derived anions more favorable. |\n| **Ranjan, S.; Sasselov, D. D. (2017)**, \u201cConstraints on the Early Terrestrial Surface UV Environment Relevant to Prebiotic Chemistry\u201d | *Astrobiology* | Establishes quantitative early-Earth UV boundary conditions for photochemical pathways. |\n| **Toner, J. D.; Catling, D. C. (2019)**, \u201cAlkaline lake settings for concentrated prebiotic cyanide and the origin of life\u201d | *Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta* | Experimental/model support for ferrocyanide accumulation and later concentrated NaCN production in closed-basin lakes. |\n| **Steinman, G.; Lemmon, R. M.; Calvin, M. (1964)**, \u201cCyanamide: A Possible Key Compound in Chemical Evolution\u201d | *PNAS* 52, 27\u201330 | Foundational experimental evidence for cyanamide as a prebiotic reagent in aqueous condensation chemistry. |\n| **Dhar, N. R.; Mukherjee, S. K. (1934)**, \u201cPhotosynthesis of Amino Acids in Vitro\u201d | *Nature* 134, 499 | Early nitrate/carbohydrate/TiO\u2082/sunlight amino-acid synthesis; historical precursor to oxidized-N photochemical frameworks. |\n| **Bahadur, K. (1954)**, \u201cPhotosynthesis of Amino-Acids from Paraformaldehyde and Potassium Nitrate\u201d | *Nature* 173, 1141 | Historical formaldehyde/nitrate photochemical framework; evidence should be weighted lightly because of early analytical methods and lack of modern Arg-specific replication. |\n\nThe central research opportunity is therefore unusually clear: **arginine itself is now less in need of another plausible retrosynthesis than of end-to-end experiments that connect the existing retrosyntheses to realistic geochemical boundary conditions and quantify free Arg rather than merely a precursor or an analytical peak**. The cyanosulfidic route supplies the most developed carbon-and-guanidino construction sequence; the Orn route supplies the cleanest late-stage transformation; H\u2082/Ni chemistry supplies the most biochemically continuous upstream network. The unresolved scientific question is whether any of those modules can be made to operate together\u2014or sequentially across connected environments\u2014at concentrations, rates, and selectivities capable of explaining an appreciable prebiotic inventory of arginine.\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.72\n- Feedback: The network has sufficient molecule and reaction nodes, is connected, and uses molecule IDs consistently. Accepting the deterministic check as ground truth, all 27 reactions conserve atoms and charge with appropriate coefficients: there are no conservation violations. Factors of eight associated with S8 are ordinary formula bookkeeping, not implausibly large stoichiometries. The formal sulfur products nevertheless do not establish actual photochemical sulfur speciation or kinetics. Connected competing sinks rxn_025 and rxn_026 are chemically plausible, and rxn_027 reflects the literature-supported ornithine lactamization challenge; their rates relative to productive chemistry remain unspecified. Literature alignment is good in distinguishing the Patel et al. aminonitrile endpoint from quantified free arginine, treating sulfite and terminal thioamide substitutions as hypotheses, and avoiding unsupported glutamate-to-ornithine or direct Fischer-Tropsch arginine claims. Reaction-level citations and exact supporting conditions are still missing. P1 provides the strongest supported scaffold, but no pathway establishes a complete operating sequence yielding quantified free arginine. The major limitations are terminal hydrolysis conditions, substrate-specific validation of proposed substitutions, cyanamide guanidination selectivity, and productive ornithine oligomerization. Environmental consistency cannot be strongly established from Surface and Biochemical labels alone: temperature, pH, concentrations, irradiation, catalyst speciation, residence times, reagent delivery and transfer losses need explicit treatment. These are evidentiary and chemical-integration limitations, not penalties for particular feedstocks, shared intermediates, pathway counts or environmental ordering. Overall, this is a credible hypothesis network rather than an approved complete synthesis network; confidence remains below the required 0.90 threshold.\n- Issues: ['This is the strongest pathway. Its carbon-skeleton construction and aldehyde-stage guanidination closely follow the experimentally supported arginine-aminonitrile chemistry summarized for Patel et al. (2015). Conversion through argininamide to free arginine is chemically conventional, but substrate-specific yields, compatible hydrolysis conditions and end-to-end recovery are not established here. Surface labels and acknowledgments of geochemical requirements do not specify the operating conditions needed to assess environmental integration.', 'Early guanidination is chemically plausible, but Patel et al. (2015) report relatively inefficient direct guanidination of beta-aminopropionitrile. Product formation in a mixture containing residual cyanamide does not by itself establish how much material follows rxn_007 rather than aldehyde-stage trapping. The downstream scaffold remains coherent, with the same unresolved free-arginine hydrolysis and environmental-integration requirements as P1.', 'The sulfite substitution is conservation-consistent and reasonably identified as a hypothesis. Ranjan et al. (2018) support favorable sulfite availability and motivate photochemical alternatives, but do not establish this substrate-specific reduction or its compatibility with cyanamide trapping. Retaining sulfide for downstream sulfur-incorporating chemistry is appropriate. Free-arginine formation still depends on unvalidated terminal hydrolysis conditions.', 'This pathway combines experimentally inefficient early guanidination with a substrate-specific sulfite photoreduction that remains unestablished in the supplied literature. General sulfite photoreductant plausibility does not demonstrate selective conversion of the guanidinated nitrile to mol_014. Connectivity and downstream carbon accounting are sound, but these entry reactions and the final hydrolysis prevent high confidence in completed synthesis.', 'The route to arginine aminonitrile retains the strongest supported scaffold. However, the demonstrated homologation-stage thioamide chemistry does not establish sulfide capture and hydrolytic sulfur replacement for this terminal alpha-aminonitrile. Both proposed reactions are balanced and chemically conceivable, but their rates, selectivity and suitable conditions are missing. This alternative therefore does not yet resolve the free-arginine endpoint gap.', 'The intermediate sequence is coherent, but productive flux depends on inefficient early guanidination and unvalidated terminal thioamide processing. Patel et al. (2015) support the broader scaffold, not an established free-arginine endpoint through rxn_018 and rxn_019. Environmental feasibility remains conditional on specifying both the photochemical stages and the subsequent sulfur-replacement and amide-hydrolysis conditions.', 'This is a connected, balanced hypothesis with unresolved chemistry at sulfite-driven entry and terminal thioamide processing. Sulfite availability does not validate rxn_004, while earlier thioamide homologation does not validate rxn_018 or rxn_019. The network correctly retains downstream sulfide requirements, so sulfite substitution should not be interpreted as eliminating the need for a local sulfide source.', 'The molecular sequence is internally consistent, but completion depends on inefficient early guanidination, unvalidated sulfite reduction of its product and unvalidated terminal thioamide processing. Explicitly labeling these transformations as hypotheses is appropriate literature alignment, but does not establish productive flux through them. The lower confidence reflects these chemical uncertainties, not pathway length or reuse of intermediates.', 'Discharge-derived ornithine followed by guanidination is a plausible strategy. The supplied volcanic-discharge literature supports ornithine occurrence, but rxn_020 is necessarily a coarse-grained mixture-production equation rather than an established selective transformation. Longo et al. (2020) support ornithine-to-arginine conversion conceptually, not quantitative free-ornithine conversion with cyanamide under the unspecified conditions here. Selectivity between the alpha- and delta-amino groups is a key target-identification issue.', 'The principal bottleneck is selective formation of the alpha-linked ornithine dimer without specified activation or drying conditions. Frenkel-Pinter et al. (2019) support substantial competing ornithine lactamization, appropriately represented by rxn_027. Longo et al. (2020) support postsynthetic peptide guanidination as a concept, but not this cyanamide protocol or its selectivity. Peptide hydrolysis to free arginine is plausible; however, the Biochemical label alone specifies neither an abiotic setting nor suitable reaction conditions.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Arginine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"This is a connected candidate synthesis network, not an experimentally validated end-to-end production process. The supplied evidence does not establish ten independent, complete prebiotic syntheses of free arginine. Accordingly, the ten pathways below are explicitly differentiated reaction sequences: eight variants of the cyanosulfidic scaffold and two ornithine-source pathways. They are not presented as ten experimentally demonstrated routes. The strongest scaffold follows Patel et al. (2015) to the arginine alpha-aminonitrile; conversion to free arginine remains an unquantified endpoint under the proposed environmental conditions. Copper-free initial photoreductions, substrate-specific sulfite substitutions, and alkaline endpoint processing are identified as hypotheses. The ornithine pathways distinguish discharge-derived material from isotope-supported heterogeneous synthesis of ornithine; neither assumes that the contested historical direct-arginine assignment is secure. Unsupported peptide assembly, terminal thioamide substitutions, and an invented glutamate-to-ornithine bridge are excluded. Unless explicitly identified as reported evidence, numerical conditions are proposed screening windows, not literature-validated operating recipes or predictions of successful conversion. Staged reagent delivery is necessary: ammonia-rich addition, cyanamide trapping, sulfide photochemistry, and hydrolysis must not be assumed compatible in one pot. Photochemical steps require measured spectral irradiance, dissolved-metal speciation, sulfur availability, and losses to abundant Fe2+; these quantities are presently unresolved. Neutral molecular formulas describe parent structures rather than dominant aqueous protonation states; sulfite and sulfate charges are explicit. S8 is a formal sulfur oxidation-product balance, not a claim that all photochemical sulfur becomes cyclooctasulfur. Discharge and heterogeneous source reactions are coarse-grained product-channel projections, not selective elementary reactions or complete mixture mass balances. No enantioenrichment is assumed. The environment_formed field gives the principal formation setting; reaction nodes specify alternative settings. No overall yield, productive residence time, or transport recovery is established.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CHN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethyne\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Acetylene\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H2/c1-2/h1-2H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"cyanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Cyanamide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2N2/c2-1-3/h2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"SO3^2-\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfite\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Sulfite ion\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"SO4^2-\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfate\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Sulfate ion\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"S8\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"cyclooctasulfur\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Elemental sulfur\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H3N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"prop-2-enenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Acrylonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H3N/c1-2-3-4/h2H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-aminopropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Beta-aminopropionitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-aminopropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Beta-aminopropionaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8N4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-(carbamimidoylamino)propanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Guanidinated beta-aminopropionitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9N3O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-imino-1,3-diazinan-4-ol\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Cyclic guanidinated C3 aldehyde hemiaminal\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H10N4O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"4-(carbamimidoylamino)-2-hydroxybutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"First guanidinated cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H12N4OS\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"4-(carbamimidoylamino)-2-hydroxybutanethioamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Guanidinated alpha-hydroxythioamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H12N4S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"4-(carbamimidoylamino)butanethioamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Deoxygenated guanidinated thioamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H11N3O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"4-(carbamimidoylamino)butanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Guanidinated C4 aldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H12N4O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"5-(carbamimidoylamino)-2-hydroxypentanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Arginine-skeleton cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H13N5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-5-(carbamimidoylamino)pentanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Arginine alpha-aminonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H15N5O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-5-(carbamimidoylamino)pentanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Argininamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_022\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H14N4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-5-(carbamimidoylamino)pentanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Arginine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C6H14N4O2/c7-4(5(11)12)2-1-3-10-6(8)9/h4H,1-3,7H2,(H,11,12)(H4,8,9,10)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_023\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Methane\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_024\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_025\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon monoxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Carbon monoxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO/c1-2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_026\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H12N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,5-diaminopentanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Ornithine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H12N2O2/c6-3-1-2-4(7)5(8)9/h4H,1-3,6-7H2,(H,8,9)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_027\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH4N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"urea\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Urea\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH4N2O/c2-1(3)4/h(H4,2,3,4)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_028\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4N4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-cyanoguanidine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Dicyandiamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_029\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H10N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3-aminopiperidin-2-one\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Ornithine delta-lactam\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_030\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H14N4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"5-amino-2-(carbamimidoylamino)pentanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"N-alpha-guanylornithine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu(I)-mediated acetylene hydrocyanation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I) cyanide-coordinated species\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm with a gas-retaining headspace\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I), with actual dissolved speciation measured\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed starting concentrations: 1-20 mM total cyanide, 1-20 mM dissolved acetylene, and 10-100 micromolar total Cu. Screen pH 6-8 and 1-24 h residence. Exclude O2 and sulfide during this entry step. Gas dissolution, HCN escape, copper precipitation, and acrylonitrile polymerization must be quantified.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Copper-mediated net addition of HCN across the acetylene triple bond forms acrylonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the documented entry to the arginine/proline branch in Patel et al. (2015). Cu(I) is assigned for its specific coupling role, not because a generic copper mineral guarantees catalysis. The supplied report does not provide an exact reproducible protocol for these numerical conditions. Cyanide accumulation remains conditional; Toner and Catling (2019) support storage and concentration mechanisms, not automatic delivery of the proposed dissolved concentrations.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia conjugate addition to acrylonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm, gas-retaining pool or headspace\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 1-10 mM acrylonitrile and 20-200 mM total ammonia/ammonium, pH 9-10.5, for 1-48 h. Establish conversion before dilution into the photochemical stage. Monitor multiple alkylation and oligomerization rather than assuming exclusive monoaddition.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia undergoes aza-Michael addition to the activated alkene, furnishing beta-aminopropionitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) support this carbon-nitrogen bond-forming step. Concentrated ammonia is a local environmental requirement, not an assumed ocean composition. Excess ammonia useful here can interfere with later cyanamide allocation, requiring actual measurements of reagent carryover.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Early guanidination of beta-aminopropionitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 1-10 mM aminonitrile and 1-50 mM cyanamide, pH 8.5-10, sampled over 1 h to 7 days. Quantify starting-material retention, cyanamide hydrolysis, and dimerization. Do not assign all downstream guanidinated product to this step when residual cyanamide remains.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The terminal amine attacks cyanamide carbon; proton transfer and tautomerization produce the substituted guanidine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) explicitly describe direct guanidination at this stage as relatively inefficient. This pathway is retained as a chemically distinct observed possibility, not as an equally productive alternative to aldehyde-stage capture. Its fractional contribution requires intermediate-resolved measurements.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu-assisted sulfidic photoreduction of beta-aminopropionitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cyanide-coordinated Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox species\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm with restricted H2S loss\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox species\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 1-10 mM substrate, 1-30 mM total dissolved sulfide, 10-100 micromolar total Cu, and pH 7-9. Use a shallow optical path of at most 1 cm. A 254 nm laboratory comparator is supported by the research; proposed environmental testing uses measured 200-280 nm irradiation at 0.01-0.1 mW/cm2 for 1-24 h. Supply cyanamide promptly for rxn_010. Determine dissolved Cu and sulfide after contact with Fe2+-bearing material.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photochemical reducing equivalents convert the nitrile to an imine-level intermediate; hydrolysis releases ammonia and beta-aminopropionaldehyde. Sulfide supplies reducing equivalents.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The transformation follows Patel et al. (2015). The stated concentrations, irradiance, and exposure times are prospective, not a reproduced branch-specific protocol. Ranjan et al. (2018) constrain attainable sulfide concentrations and UV transmission; Ranjan and Sasselov (2017) support surface UV availability but not equivalence between sunlight and a 254 nm lamp. Copper sulfide precipitation and FeS formation can remove the assumed active reagents.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu-assisted photoreduction of the early guanidinated nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cyanide-coordinated Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox species\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm with restricted H2S loss\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox species\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 0.1-10 mM guanidinated nitrile, 1-30 mM dissolved sulfide, 10-100 micromolar total Cu, pH 7-9, and 1-24 h irradiation. Use a 254 nm comparator and separately measured 200-280 nm surface-like exposure. Resolve the cyclic hemiaminal from residual starting material and products formed by aldehyde-stage cyanamide capture.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrile photoreduction and hydrolysis generate a guanidinated aldehyde that can close intramolecularly to a six-membered hemiaminal.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) support the early-guanidination branch within the connected chemistry, but mixed reaction products do not establish its isolated flux or efficiency. The cyclic node represents an aldehyde-equivalent structure; its tautomer and stereoisomer distribution is not assigned an unverified InChI. Sulfur and copper availability remain subject to the constraints discussed by Ranjan et al. (2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Copper-free sulfidic entry photoreduction\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm with restricted H2S loss\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 1-10 mM aminonitrile, 5-30 mM sulfide, pH 7-9, and 1-48 h irradiation. Compare a 254 nm lamp with measured 200-280 nm exposure at 0.01-0.1 mW/cm2. Verify removal of copper carried from rxn_001; do not assume natural quantitative metal separation. Later photochemical steps in this pathway may still require Cu.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Direct sulfide photochemistry is proposed to provide reducing equivalents for nitrile-to-aldehyde conversion without added copper.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative for this substrate under these conditions. Patel et al. (2015) and the supplied research indicate that some related sulfidic reductions can occur without copper, but that does not establish this arginine-branch reaction's rate or selectivity. The alternative has chemical merit where copper precipitates, but only if sufficient unscavenged sulfide and usable photons remain; Ranjan et al. (2018) identify that supply constraint.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Copper-free photoreduction of the early guanidinated nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm with restricted H2S loss\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 0.1-10 mM substrate, 5-30 mM sulfide, pH 7-9, and 1-48 h irradiation with the same measured spectral comparison used for rxn_006. Measure guanidino-group retention, substrate disappearance, and hemiaminal formation independently.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Proposed direct sulfidic photoreduction of the nitrile is followed by hydrolysis and intramolecular hemiaminal formation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative substrate-specific extension of related copper-free photochemistry discussed around Patel et al. (2015). Neither the inefficient early guanidination nor general availability of photochemical electrons demonstrates this edge. It is retained as a testable catalyst-dependence alternative, not as a demonstrated improvement.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfite-driven entry photoreduction\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 1-10 mM aminonitrile, 1-30 mM total sulfite/bisulfite, pH 8-9, and 1-48 h irradiation. Compare 254 nm irradiation with measured 200-280 nm surface-like exposure. Exclude O2 and measure carbonyl-bisulfite trapping, hydrated-electron scavenging, sulfate formation, and cyanamide compatibility.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sulfite photoionization is proposed to supply reducing equivalents; nitrile reduction and hydrolysis produce the aldehyde. Sulfate provides a balanced net oxidation endpoint for the sulfur donor.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative for beta-aminopropionitrile. Ranjan et al. (2018) support potentially more favorable sulfite availability and motivate alternative sulfur photochemistry, but do not validate this substrate conversion or its product distribution. Sulfate is a net balance, not a claim of exclusive sulfur speciation. Sulfide is still required downstream for thioamide formation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfite-driven photoreduction of the early guanidinated nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 0.1-10 mM guanidinated nitrile, 1-30 mM sulfite/bisulfite, pH 8-9, and 1-48 h measured UV exposure. Determine whether carbonyl trapping, guanidino photochemistry, or sulfur-radical addition competes with the intended conversion.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Proposed sulfite-derived reducing equivalents convert the nitrile to an aldehyde equivalent, followed by cyclic hemiaminal formation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative. Ranjan et al. (2018) provide environmental motivation, not substrate-specific experimental support. Patel et al. (2015) support the surrounding scaffold but not this substitution. This pathway therefore carries both the early-guanidination bottleneck and an unvalidated photoreduction.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Aldehyde-stage cyanamide capture and guanidino hemiaminal formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 0.1-10 mM freshly generated aldehyde and 1-30 mM cyanamide, pH 7-9. Introduce cyanamide during or immediately after aldehyde generation; sample from minutes to 24 h. Quantify aldehyde degradation, hemiaminal formation, and remaining cyanamide before further HCN addition.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanamide engages the aldehyde and the tethered amine in a coupled addition/cyclization sequence, furnishing a guanidinated cyclic hemiaminal that acts as an open-chain aldehyde reservoir.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the better-supported guanidination timing in Patel et al. (2015), where photogenerated aldehyde capture was much more effective than direct guanidination of the aminonitrile. The paper's qualitative rapid trapping does not establish a numerical rate constant for the proposed pool composition. Maintaining cyanamide availability despite rxn_024 and rxn_025 is a necessary kinetic condition.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"First cyanide homologation of the guanidinated aldehyde equivalent\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm with restricted HCN loss\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 0.1-10 mM hemiaminal and 1-20 mM total cyanide, pH 7.5-9, for 0.5-24 h in a dark or shielded mixing compartment. Measure the reversible ring-opening/cyanohydrin equilibrium rather than assuming complete conversion.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible opening of the cyclic hemiaminal exposes the aldehyde; cyanide addition and proton transfer extend the carbon skeleton by one carbon.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) support HCN homologation of the guanidinated aldehyde-derived branch. Explicit ring opening avoids treating the isolated hemiaminal as though it were an unencumbered free aldehyde. Local cyanide concentration and retention are additional requirements, consistent with the motivation for storage mechanisms in Toner and Catling (2019).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfide addition to form the alpha-hydroxythioamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm with restricted H2S loss\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 0.1-10 mM cyanohydrin and 5-30 mM dissolved sulfide, pH 7-9, for 1-48 h. Begin without irradiation to distinguish sulfur incorporation from later photochemistry. Determine sulfide consumption by Fe2+ and copper before interpreting nominal reagent concentrations.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrosulfide attacks the nitrile carbon; proton transfers produce the alpha-hydroxythioamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This sulfur-incorporating step belongs to the demonstrated homologation chemistry in Patel et al. (2015). Sulfite cannot be substituted merely because it can sometimes supply photochemical electrons: a reduced sulfur atom is incorporated here. Ranjan et al. (2018) therefore remain relevant even for pathways using sulfite at the entry reduction.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Photochemical deoxygenation of the alpha-hydroxythioamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox species\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm with restricted H2S loss\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox species, where maintained in an active form\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 0.1-10 mM hydroxythioamide, 1-30 mM sulfide, 10-100 micromolar total Cu, pH 7-9, and 1-24 h irradiation. Compare a 254 nm laboratory benchmark with measured surface-like irradiation. Monitor thioamide cleavage, guanidino retention, sulfur turbidity, and optical attenuation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photoreductive C-O cleavage replaces the alpha-hydroxyl group with hydrogen, retaining the thioamide needed for subsequent chain extension.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) support this hydroxythioamide-to-thioamide homologation stage. The balanced S8 output represents the reductant oxidation requirement, not demonstrated sulfur-product selectivity. Neither a constant photon dose nor constant dissolved copper can be presumed as sulfur precipitates accumulate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Thioamide photoreduction and hydrolysis to the homologated aldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox species\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm with restricted H2S loss\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox species\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 0.1-10 mM thioamide, a maintained 1-30 mM sulfide pool, 10-100 micromolar total Cu, pH 7-9, and 1-24 h irradiation. Sample aldehyde formation before adding the next HCN pulse. Determine actual sulfur speciation and ammonia release.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A lumped photoreduction/hydrolysis module converts the thioamide carbon into an aldehyde. H2S is included on both sides to represent reductant participation followed by sulfide release from the substrate; cancellation gives the formal net sulfur-extrusion balance.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The further reduction to an aldehyde equivalent follows the homologation sequence described by Patel et al. (2015). This reaction node does not claim that the written net balance identifies every elementary intermediate or the measured sulfur distribution. Treating it as a module is more defensible than inventing isolated mechanistic intermediates not established in the supplied research.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Second cyanide homologation to the arginine carbon skeleton\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm with restricted HCN loss\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 0.1-10 mM aldehyde and 1-20 mM total cyanide, pH 7.5-9, for 0.5-24 h after the photoreduction stage. Limit further irradiation during addition and quantify reversible cyanohydrin formation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide addition to the homologated aldehyde establishes the five-carbon amino-acid backbone; the guanidino carbon accounts for the sixth carbon of arginine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) support the second HCN addition in the arginine-precursor branch. Explicit carbon accounting distinguishes backbone homologation from guanidino-group installation and avoids an unexplained jump in molecular size.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker-type conversion of the final cyanohydrin to arginine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm with ammonia retention\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 0.1-10 mM cyanohydrin and 20-200 mM total ammonia/ammonium, pH 8.5-10, for 1-48 h. Use a dark mixing stage after photochemistry. Quantify aminonitrile, residual cyanohydrin, and cyclic or hydrolyzed products.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Through reversible carbonyl/imine chemistry and cyanide capture, ammonia replaces the cyanohydrin hydroxyl functionality in the net reaction, yielding the alpha-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) identify compound 46 as the arginine alpha-aminonitrile precursor. This is the experimentally strongest arginine-specific endpoint in the supplied de novo literature. It must not be equated with an isolated or quantitatively recovered free-arginine product.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Acidic hydration of arginine aminonitrile to argininamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium from an anoxic volcanic acid source\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 0.1-10 mM aminonitrile, pH 1-2 maintained by volcanic acid input, and sampling over 1 h to 30 days. This is a separate shielded hydrolysis stage after photochemistry. Measure retro-Strecker loss, amide accumulation, guanidino retention, and total nitrogen balance. No productive residence time is established.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid activation of the nitrile permits water addition and tautomerization to the corresponding primary amide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Conventional nitrile chemistry supports this mechanistic expectation, but the proposed environmental operating step is unvalidated. Patel et al. (2015) establish the aminonitrile precursor, not a substrate-specific hydration rate or argininamide yield under these conditions. Mild volcanic acidity is specified rather than silently assuming concentrated laboratory acid; whether it is sufficient before competing decomposition is a central bottleneck.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"Acidic hydrolysis of argininamide to free arginine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium from an anoxic volcanic acid source\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Maintain pH 1-2 and sample 0.1-10 mM amide over 1-30 days, followed by cooling to 300-320 K. Ammonia is predominantly ammonium in situ. Quantify free arginine against an authentic standard and independently measure ornithine, urea, remaining amide, and other degradation products. Recovery during neutralization or concentration is unknown.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-catalyzed amide hydrolysis gives the carboxylic acid functionality and releases ammonia/ammonium.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Chemically conventional but unvalidated as this network's geochemical endpoint. The supplied Patel et al. (2015) evidence does not provide an end-to-end isolated free-arginine yield. Specifying a plausible acid pool does not establish favorable competition between amide hydrolysis, substrate destruction, and dilution. Both rxn_017 and rxn_018 require direct kinetic and isotope-dilution product measurements.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_019\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkaline hydrothermal hydration of arginine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide supplied by alkaline water-rock chemistry\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-400 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-50 bar, maintaining liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide; no mineral-specific catalytic enhancement assumed\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 0.1-10 mM aminonitrile and pH 10-11 referenced to a cooled 298 K aliquot. Screen 1-30 days in a UV-shielded alkaline pore or flow-through zone. Transport from surface photochemistry must be measured; no concentration preservation is assumed. Track racemization, nitrile loss, guanidino degradation, and amide formation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide-promoted nitrile hydration produces the amide, written in neutral-parent molecular bookkeeping.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative operating alternative based on general nitrile hydrolysis chemistry, not an experiment reported by Patel et al. (2015). It has a distinct environmental rationale where alkaline heated water is available but strong acidity is not. Its disadvantages are slow hydration at modest alkalinity and potentially accelerated destruction of the desired functionality. It is not evidence that a submarine vent can perform the UV-dependent upstream sequence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_020\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkaline hydrothermal hydrolysis of argininamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide supplied by alkaline water-rock chemistry\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-400 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-50 bar, maintaining liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide; alkalinity must be replenished\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 0.1-10 mM amide, cooled-aliquot pH 10-11, and 1-30 days of sampling. Product is predominantly carboxylate in situ. Cool and remove product from prolonged heating where possible, while measuring recovery rather than assuming it. Reject this branch if guanidino loss or other decomposition outruns amide hydrolysis.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide-promoted acyl substitution releases ammonia and generates the amino-acid carboxylate; the displayed formula is the neutral parent.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative as a selective prebiotic endpoint. No supplied study validates argininamide-to-arginine conversion under this alkaline hydrothermal window. Patel et al. (2015) support the upstream precursor only. Alkaline processing is therefore a testable alternative, not a resolution of the missing free-arginine demonstration.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_021\",\n      \"name\": \"Volcanic discharge production and aqueous recovery of an ornithine-containing fraction\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_026\", \"mol_024\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370 K bulk gas-water system; transient discharge channels are much hotter\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic methane/ammonia/water-vapor discharge with hot-water mist and a 300-330 K condensate collector. Proposed source-screening duration is 1-7 days; no particular gas ratio or ornithine concentration is established by the supplied summary. This node includes product collection and any necessary analytical hydrolysis. Downstream guanidination requires measured ornithine recovery and concentration, not direct use of an uncharacterized tar mixture.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Coarse-grained discharge-driven radical chemistry, carbon-chain growth, nitrogen incorporation, condensation, and aqueous processing generate a broad mixture containing an ornithine channel. The displayed balance is not an elementary methane-to-ornithine reaction.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Johnson et al. (2008) support the expanded amino-acid inventory of the volcanic-discharge apparatus; the supplied review attributes ornithine occurrence to that work and subsequent analyses. This supports a possible ornithine source, not a selective route, a measured environmental yield, or a demonstrated connection to cyanamide guanidination. Unresolved intermediates are not invented to imply mechanistic completeness.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_022\",\n      \"name\": \"Heterogeneous CO/H2/NH3 production and recovery of an ornithine-containing fraction\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_025\", \"mol_024\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_026\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Native nickel-bearing catalytic surface\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"473-600 K proposed gas-solid stage, followed by 300-330 K collection\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-50 bar proposed terrestrial adaptation; the supplied research summary does not specify the historical experimental pressure\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Nickel-bearing surface with accessible reduced metal\",\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic CO/H2/NH3 flow over a characterized metal surface, with immediate transfer to a cooler collector and aqueous workup. Screen 1-24 h hot-zone exposure without assuming an established optimum. The historical experiments covered 473-973 K; ornithine formation specifically within the proposed narrower range and elevated pressure is unvalidated. Sulfur poisoning, competing hydrocarbons, and amino-acid survival must be measured.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Unresolved heterogeneous carbon reduction/coupling and nitrogen-incorporation chemistry generates a complex product mixture. The written reaction is a formal ornithine-producing channel, not a selective Fischer-Tropsch elementary step.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Yoshino et al. (1971) reported broad amino-acid synthesis, and Hayatsu et al. (1971) included ornithine among isotope/mass-spectrometry-confirmed products. Arginine was not in that confirmed list, so this node deliberately supplies ornithine rather than asserting direct arginine synthesis. The high-pressure terrestrial mapping and lower-temperature subset are speculative adaptations, and the historical very low amino-acid yields prevent assuming useful downstream concentrations.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_023\",\n      \"name\": \"Free-ornithine delta-amine guanidination by cyanamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_026\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 0.1-10 mM analytically verified ornithine and 1-50 mM cyanamide, pH 9-10.5, sampled over 6 h to 7 days. Compare limited cyanamide pulses with excess reagent. Quantify alpha- versus delta-guanidination, overguanidination, cyanamide loss, lactam formation, and effects of Fe2+, salts, and residual ammonia from source mixtures. Concentration and source-to-pool transfer recovery are unknown.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The ornithine delta-amine attacks cyanamide carbon, followed by proton transfer and tautomerization to the arginine guanidino group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Longo et al. (2020) strongly support chemical ornithine-to-arginine conversion in peptide contexts and discuss simple guanidinating reagents, but their complete peptide conversion is not evidence for this free-ornithine/cyanamide environmental protocol. Steinman et al. (1964) establish cyanamide's broader prebiotic reactivity, not a quantitative arginine yield. Free ornithine has two competing amino groups, so selective production of arginine is a central unresolved requirement rather than an assumed outcome.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_024\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing cyanamide hydrolysis\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_027\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370 K across relevant pool stages\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Monitor alongside productive reactions over their actual pH and residence-time histories, with proposed initial cyanamide concentrations of 1-50 mM. Sample from hours to weeks; do not assign one universal hydrolysis rate across acidic, neutral, and alkaline stages.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water addition to cyanamide and proton transfers form urea.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This chemically established competing sink is included because cyanamide is not an inexhaustible reagent. Steinman et al. (1964) support its finite aqueous lifetime and competing reactivity in chemical-evolution chemistry, but the supplied literature does not determine the sink rate in the proposed arginine mixtures. Rate competition must be measured rather than inferred from thermodynamic favorability.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_025\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing cyanamide dimerization\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_028\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350 K; proposed monitoring window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Track during cyanamide concentration and guanidination at pH 8-10.5, particularly with 10-50 mM or more concentrated cyanamide. Sample over 1 h to 7 days and through partial evaporation. The cyanamide coefficient is inferred rather than represented by repeating its molecule ID.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"One cyanamide molecule adds to another, giving cyanoguanidine, commonly called dicyandiamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"A plausible and chemically established cyanamide sink, included to prevent unlimited allocation of the same reagent to productive branches. The supplied report identifies dicyandiamide formation as a necessary measurement, but provides no applicable rate constants. Steinman et al. (1964) support cyanamide's competing condensation chemistry; they do not validate this network's predicted partitioning.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_026\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing ornithine delta-lactam formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_026\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_029\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K; proposed dry-down screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Partially dry ornithine-containing films formed from initially pH 7-9 solutions. Proposed comparison: 1-3 wet-dry cycles with 8-24 h dry phases. These times are not asserted to reproduce the cited protocol. Measure lactamization during any source-concentration step rather than treating evaporation as loss-free.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Intramolecular attack of the delta-amine on the carboxyl group eliminates water and forms a six-membered lactam.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Frenkel-Pinter et al. (2019) demonstrate the importance of ornithine lactamization during prebiotic dry-down chemistry. This connected sink is why the network does not manufacture an unsupported productive ornithine-peptide route merely to increase the pathway count. A useful ornithine-first pathway must outcompete or avoid this loss during concentration.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_027\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing alpha-amine guanidination of free ornithine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_026\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_030\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K; proposed screening window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Use the same 0.1-10 mM ornithine, 1-50 mM cyanamide, pH 9-10.5, and 6 h to 7 day time course as rxn_023. Separate regioisomers chromatographically and confirm connectivity; exact mass alone cannot distinguish this product from arginine.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The alpha-amine, rather than the delta-amine, attacks cyanamide to produce the constitutional guanidination isomer.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative competing channel included on functional-group chemistry grounds; no supplied study quantifies it for free ornithine plus cyanamide. Longo et al. (2020) demonstrate useful selectivity in particular peptide substrates, but those observations cannot be transferred quantitatively to an unprotected free diamino acid. Explicitly representing this isomer prevents equating a C6H14N4O2 analytical signal with confirmed arginine.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Aldehyde-stage guanidination with Cu/sulfide photochemistry and acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Strongest supplied de novo scaffold: construct beta-aminopropionaldehyde, trap it with cyanamide, homologate through thioamides, and form arginine aminonitrile. Acidic hydration and amide hydrolysis are chemically conventional but unvalidated under the proposed pool conditions. This is not a demonstrated complete free-arginine synthesis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_018\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_021\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Early guanidination with Cu/sulfide photochemistry and acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Guanidination precedes the first nitrile reduction. This differs from P1 in reaction order and intermediate identity, but Patel et al. report inefficient early guanidination. Its productive contribution must be distinguished from subsequent aldehyde capture by residual cyanamide. The acidic free-arginine endpoint remains unvalidated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_018\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_021\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Copper-free entry reduction followed by aldehyde-stage guanidination\",\n      \"description\": \"Tests whether direct sulfidic photochemistry can replace Cu assistance for the initial aminonitrile reduction. Only that entry reduction is copper-free; the acetylene coupling and subsequent photochemical stages still use Cu. Substrate-specific conversion and environmental metal separation are unresolved, in addition to the terminal hydrolysis gap.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_018\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_021\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Early guanidination followed by copper-free entry reduction\",\n      \"description\": \"Combines the experimentally inefficient early guanidination branch with a speculative copper-free reduction of its product. The remaining homologation sequence is shared with the supported scaffold. Neither the proposed reduction nor quantitative free-arginine recovery has been demonstrated.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_018\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_021\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfite entry reduction followed by aldehyde-stage guanidination\",\n      \"description\": \"Uses a speculative sulfite-driven reduction motivated by environmental sulfite availability. Sulfide remains necessary for thioamide formation and later sulfidic photochemistry. Carbonyl-bisulfite trapping and compatibility with cyanamide are additional bottlenecks; favorable sulfur supply alone does not validate the route.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_018\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_021\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Early guanidination followed by sulfite entry reduction\",\n      \"description\": \"Tests sulfite photoreduction of the guanidinated nitrile rather than the unmodified aminonitrile. It is a distinct substrate/reductant hypothesis, not an experimentally established route. It combines inefficient early guanidination, unvalidated substrate-specific photochemistry, and the unresolved acidic free-arginine endpoint.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_018\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_021\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Aldehyde-stage guanidination followed by alkaline hydrothermal endpoint processing\",\n      \"description\": \"Shares the strongest surface precursor scaffold with P1 but replaces acidic terminal hydrolysis with two alkaline hydrothermal transformations. These are different reaction conditions and catalytic channels, not a new carbon-skeleton synthesis. Transport losses and competition between hydrolysis and guanidino degradation must be measured before claiming completion.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_019\", \"rxn_020\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_021\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Early guanidination followed by alkaline hydrothermal endpoint processing\",\n      \"description\": \"Combines early guanidination with the alkaline endpoint alternative. It is distinguished from P2 by the hydrolysis regime and from P7 by guanidination timing. Both the inefficient entry branch and the selective alkaline production of free arginine require validation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_019\", \"rxn_020\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_021\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Volcanic-discharge ornithine followed by free-monomer guanidination\",\n      \"description\": \"Uses a literature-supported abiotic ornithine source class, followed by the chemically plausible cyanamide guanidination cut. The source node is a mixture-generating module, not selective ornithine synthesis. Ornithine recovery, concentration without lactamization, and delta- versus alpha-guanidination are unresolved; Longo et al.'s peptide results do not close these gaps.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_021\", \"rxn_023\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_026\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Heterogeneously synthesized ornithine followed by free-monomer guanidination\",\n      \"description\": \"Uses isotope-supported historical ornithine formation from CO/H2/NH3 rather than the contested direct-arginine assignment. The proposed terrestrial pressure and temperature subset are unvalidated adaptations. Very low source selectivity, hot-zone survival, collection efficiency, and selective cyanamide guanidination prevent treating this as an established complete synthesis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_022\", \"rxn_023\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_026\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_011\",\n    \"mol_012\",\n    \"mol_013\",\n    \"mol_014\",\n    \"mol_020\",\n    \"mol_021\",\n    \"mol_026\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_016\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_017\", \"rxn_019\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_017\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_018\", \"rxn_020\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_026\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_021\", \"rxn_022\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_023\", \"rxn_026\", \"rxn_027\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_022\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_018\", \"rxn_020\", \"rxn_023\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Arginine", "overall_strategy": "This is a connected candidate synthesis network, not an experimentally validated end-to-end production process. The supplied evidence does not establish ten independent, complete prebiotic syntheses of free arginine. Accordingly, the ten pathways below are explicitly differentiated reaction sequences: eight variants of the cyanosulfidic scaffold and two ornithine-source pathways. They are not presented as ten experimentally demonstrated routes. The strongest scaffold follows Patel et al. (2015) to the arginine alpha-aminonitrile; conversion to free arginine remains an unquantified endpoint under the proposed environmental conditions. Copper-free initial photoreductions, substrate-specific sulfite substitutions, and alkaline endpoint processing are identified as hypotheses. The ornithine pathways distinguish discharge-derived material from isotope-supported heterogeneous synthesis of ornithine; neither assumes that the contested historical direct-arginine assignment is secure. Unsupported peptide assembly, terminal thioamide substitutions, and an invented glutamate-to-ornithine bridge are excluded. Unless explicitly identified as reported evidence, numerical conditions are proposed screening windows, not literature-validated operating recipes or predictions of successful conversion. Staged reagent delivery is necessary: ammonia-rich addition, cyanamide trapping, sulfide photochemistry, and hydrolysis must not be assumed compatible in one pot. Photochemical steps require measured spectral irradiance, dissolved-metal speciation, sulfur availability, and losses to abundant Fe2+; these quantities are presently unresolved. Neutral molecular formulas describe parent structures rather than dominant aqueous protonation states; sulfite and sulfate charges are explicit. S8 is a formal sulfur oxidation-product balance, not a claim that all photochemical sulfur becomes cyclooctasulfur. Discharge and heterogeneous source reactions are coarse-grained product-channel projections, not selective elementary reactions or complete mixture mass balances. No enantioenrichment is assumed. The environment_formed field gives the principal formation setting; reaction nodes specify alternative settings. No overall yield, productive residence time, or transport recovery is established.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "C2H2", "iupac_name": "ethyne", "common_name": "Acetylene", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2/c1-2/h1-2H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "CH2N2", "iupac_name": "cyanamide", "common_name": "Cyanamide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2N2/c2-1-3/h2H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "sulfane", "common_name": "Hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "SO3^2-", "iupac_name": "sulfite", "common_name": "Sulfite ion", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "SO4^2-", "iupac_name": "sulfate", "common_name": "Sulfate ion", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "S8", "iupac_name": "cyclooctasulfur", "common_name": "Elemental sulfur", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C3H3N", "iupac_name": "prop-2-enenitrile", "common_name": "Acrylonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H3N/c1-2-3-4/h2H,1H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C3H6N2", "iupac_name": "3-aminopropanenitrile", "common_name": "Beta-aminopropionitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C3H7NO", "iupac_name": "3-aminopropanal", "common_name": "Beta-aminopropionaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C4H8N4", "iupac_name": "3-(carbamimidoylamino)propanenitrile", "common_name": "Guanidinated beta-aminopropionitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C4H9N3O", "iupac_name": "2-imino-1,3-diazinan-4-ol", "common_name": "Cyclic guanidinated C3 aldehyde hemiaminal", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C5H10N4O", "iupac_name": "4-(carbamimidoylamino)-2-hydroxybutanenitrile", "common_name": "First guanidinated cyanohydrin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C5H12N4OS", "iupac_name": "4-(carbamimidoylamino)-2-hydroxybutanethioamide", "common_name": "Guanidinated alpha-hydroxythioamide", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "C5H12N4S", "iupac_name": "4-(carbamimidoylamino)butanethioamide", "common_name": "Deoxygenated guanidinated thioamide", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "C5H11N3O", "iupac_name": "4-(carbamimidoylamino)butanal", "common_name": "Guanidinated C4 aldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "C6H12N4O", "iupac_name": "5-(carbamimidoylamino)-2-hydroxypentanenitrile", "common_name": "Arginine-skeleton cyanohydrin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "C6H13N5", "iupac_name": "2-amino-5-(carbamimidoylamino)pentanenitrile", "common_name": "Arginine alpha-aminonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "C6H15N5O", "iupac_name": "2-amin