ivity for beta-aminopropionitrile reduction in rxn_006 under the proposed setting. The network correctly labels this substitution as hypothetical. Subsequent aldehyde trapping and homologation follow the stronger scaffold, but cannot compensate for an unvalidated entry reduction. This is not a wholly copper-free pathway because rxn_001 still requires Cu(I). Acidic endpoint recovery is also unresolved.', 'This pathway combines inefficient early guanidination with an unvalidated copper-free reduction of the guanidinated nitrile. General evidence for sulfide photoreduction does not establish the substrate-specific behavior of mol_013, including its protonation, competing reactions, and productive hemiaminal formation. The later transformations remain chemically coherent, but the pathway lacks evidence that sufficient material reaches them. Free arginine is still an expected rather than demonstrated endpoint under the stated conditions.', 'Ranjan et al. (2018) support the potential environmental availability of sulfite, but do not establish rxn_008 on this substrate. Its balanced redox equation is necessary but insufficient evidence for useful photochemical conversion. Sulfite/bisulfite interactions with the generated aldehyde may also affect cyanamide trapping. This substitution does not eliminate the reduced-sulfur requirements of the later thioamide chemistry. The final acidic hydrolysis sequence remains unquantified.', 'The conserved stoichiometry of rxn_009 does not validate sulfite-driven reduction of the early-guanylated nitrile. This substrate-specific substitution is unsupported by the supplied experimental findings and follows an already inefficient guanidination step. The environmental motivation for sulfite is reasonable, but successful reduction, hemiaminal formation, and compatibility with subsequent sulfide chemistry remain hypotheses. The pathway correctly avoids claiming that sulfite replaces sulfur incorporation throughout the network.', 'The pathway retains the strongest precursor-forming scaffold, but alkaline hydrothermal processing introduces an unvalidated endpoint. Base-promoted nitrile and amide hydrolysis are chemically possible; however, no temperature-pH-residence-time window demonstrates selective arginine accumulation rather than competing substrate or product degradation. The concern is not the use of another environment, but the absence of specified phase conditions, transport recovery, and substrate-specific hydrolysis selectivity.', 'This pathway combines the material-throughput uncertainty of early guanidination with the unvalidated alkaline hydrothermal endpoint. Neither issue violates conservation, and the formal sequence reaches the correct target structure. Nevertheless, evidence for productive passage through rxn_003/rxn_005 and selective completion of rxn_019/rxn_020 is insufficient to establish free-arginine production. Environmental consistency cannot be verified from the broad setting labels alone.', 'The supplied volcanic-discharge literature supports an abiotic ornithine source, but not a selective source reaction or quantified delivery to guanidination. Cyanamide-mediated amine guanidination is plausible, yet Longo et al. (2020) do not establish selective free-ornithine-to-arginine conversion under these conditions. Free ornithine has competing alpha and delta amines. Including cyanamide losses, lactamization, and N-alpha-guanylornithine is appropriate, but does not establish their rates or the target selectivity.', 'Hayatsu, Studier and Anders (1971) provide isotope-supported evidence for heterogeneous ornithine synthesis, so this pathway appropriately avoids relying on the contested direct-arginine assignment. However, that evidence does not establish selective ornithine production in an unspecified hydrothermal aqueous setting. The reported hot heterogeneous chemistry requires explicit catalyst, phase, pressure, collection, and workup conditions. Subsequent free-monomer guanidination retains the selectivity and reagent-competition uncertainties of P9.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Arginine, without specified stereochemistry\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"This is a literature-constrained candidate synthesis network, not an experimentally completed synthesis or a claim that ten validated prebiotic routes exist. Ten distinct reaction sequences are enumerated: eight variants of the cyanosulfidic scaffold and two independently sourced ornithine routes. The eight scaffold variants test chemically relevant differences in guanidination timing, copper availability during the first photoreduction, and terminal hydrolysis conditions; they are not eight independent discoveries. Patel et al. (2015) provide the strongest evidence through the arginine alpha-aminonitrile, not quantitatively recovered free arginine. Neither selective endpoint hydrolysis nor environmental free-ornithine guanidination is established by the supplied evidence. All numerical operating windows below are proposed experimental screening conditions unless explicitly identified as reported observations; missing literature protocols, rates, yields, and transport recoveries are not invented. Sequential reagent delivery is essential. For every transfer, downstream concentration must be calculated as recovered moles divided by receiving volume, with recovery measured rather than assumed quantitative. No overall yield can presently be assigned. Neutral parent formulas are used consistently; actual protonation depends on pH. S8 is an idealized terminal sulfur balance product, not an assertion that it is the primary photoproduct. Non-target sink species use the schema's intermediate role because no byproduct role is available. environment_formed identifies a representative formation setting, not an exclusive one. All stages are ano