m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Arginine, without specified stereochemistry\nOVERALL STRATEGY: This is a literature-constrained candidate synthesis network, not an experimentally completed synthesis or a claim that ten validated prebiotic routes exist. Ten distinct reaction sequences are enumerated: eight variants of the cyanosulfidic scaffold and two independently sourced ornithine routes. The eight scaffold variants test chemically relevant differences in guanidination timing, copper availability during the first photoreduction, and terminal hydrolysis conditions; they are not eight independent discoveries. Patel et al. (2015) provide the strongest evidence through the arginine alpha-aminonitrile, not quantitatively recovered free arginine. Neither selective endpoint hydrolysis nor environmental free-ornithine guanidination is established by the supplied evidence. All numerical operating windows below are proposed experimental screening conditions unless explicitly identified as reported observations; missing literature protocols, rates, yields, and transport recoveries are not invented. Sequential reagent delivery is essential. For every transfer, downstream concentration must be calculated as recovered moles divided by receiving volume, with recovery measured rather than