tified.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_017\",\n        \"rxn_018\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Establish product-specific conversion and selectivity for each distinguishing reaction before attempting the complete sequence.\",\n        \"Use copper-containing and aldehyde-stage-trapping controls to identify which chemistry actually carries productive flux.\",\n        \"Measure intermediate survival and target recovery under the proposed alkaline conditions rather than inferring them from formal hydrolysis stoichiometry.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_019\": 0.65,\n        \"rxn_021\": 0.49\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.61,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.64,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.51,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.46,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.55,\n      \"feedback\": \"Volcanic-discharge chemistry provides evidence for an abiotic ornithine source, but rxn_019 is an aggregate product-source channel rather than a resolved selective synthesis. Its balanced equation does not establish the concentration or recovery of free ornithine available downstream. Cyanamide guanidination is mechanistically plausible, but delta-selectivity on free ornithine is not demonstrated by Longo et al.'s peptide experiments. The explicit alpha-guanylation, cyanamide-loss, and lactamization sinks are appropriate acknowledgments of competition, not evidence that the arginine-forming branch dominates.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_019\",\n        \"rxn_021\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Quantify free ornithine in collected discharge products and distinguish it from ornithine released only during analytical workup.\",\n        \"Measure free-ornithine guanidination across defined pH and cyanamide concentrations, resolving arginine from N-alpha-guanylornithine.\",\n        \"Demonstrate rainout, collection, and concentration with measured recovery, including lactamization and cyanamide consumption where drying is used.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_020\": 0.61,\n        \"rxn_021\": 0.49\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.58,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.6,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.41,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.44,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.51,\n      \"feedback\": \"The supplied isotope-confirmation literature supports ornithine from heterogeneous CO/H2/NH3 chemistry, so this pathway should not inherit the separate uncertainty surrounding the historical direct-arginine assignment. Nevertheless, rxn_020 remains a low-selectivity aggregate source with unresolved intermediates and recovery. The cited 200-700 degree Celsius mineral/gas experiments are not adequately specified by the Hydrothermal label: catalyst, phase, pressure, cooling, and collection must be distinguished. Downstream delta-selective cyanamide guanidination of free ornithine remains unvalidated.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_020\",\n        \"rxn_021\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify whether rxn_020 represents a gas-solid reactor, a hot mineral interface, or an aqueous hydrothermal system, and justify any departure from the cited experiments.\",\n        \"Measure ornithine-specific production, release, survival during cooling, and downstream aqueous recovery rather than using total amino-acid yield.\",\n        \"Validate cyanamide guanidination on the recovered mixed product stream and distinguish arginine from constitutional isomers.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"best_pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n  \"overall_confidence\": 0.7,\n  \"network_feedback\": \"The network is structurally valid and scientifically useful as a candidate experimental program, but it is not approved as a demonstrated complete synthesis network because confidence remains below 0.90. Its 28 molecule nodes and 26 reaction nodes form a connected network, molecule IDs are consistent, and the deterministic check establishes conservation for every reaction: 17 are balanced as written and nine are balanceable with coefficients. There are no UNBALANCED reactions. Coefficients of eight associated with S8 are not intrinsically implausible. In rxn_012, H2S cancels in the displayed inferred balance; the formal sulfur bookkeeping should not be mistaken for a measured photochemical mechanism or sulfur-product distribution. Sink nodes and unspecified target stereochemistry are not defects. The principal strength is the literature-supported construction of the arginine alpha-aminonitrile, especially through aldehyde-stage cyanamide trapping. The submission correctly distinguishes this achievement from quantitative recovery of free arginine and does not misrepresent peptide guanidination as an environmental free-monomer experiment. However, all eight scaffold variants share an unvalidated finishing module, and both ornithine routes share unvalidated free-ornithine guanidination selectivity. Their enumeration does not provide independent confirmation of target achievement. Reactions rxn_022 and rxn_023 represent chemically reasonable cyanamide sinks; rxn_024 addresses literature-supported ornithine lactamization; rxn_025 acknowledges plausible competing alpha-guanylation; and rxn_026 identifies a possible arginine-loss channel. Their rates and importance under the proposed conditions remain unknown, and the literature-described unguanidinated/proline-precursor branch is not explicitly represented. Environmental assessment is limited because this representation supplies no numerical reaction windows, catalyst loadings, UV exposures, residence times, or concrete transfer protocols. Recovered moles divided by receiving volume is correct accounting, but it does not establish a physically viable transfer. Copper availability, sulfide concentration, UV penetration, and reagent sequencing require joint demonstration rather than separate assertions. Literature alignment is substantively good, although reaction-level citations and protocol mappings should be added, particularly for copper-free variants and the ornithine source channels. The decisive next result is an integrated experiment that establishes intermediate identities, resolves arginine from N-alpha-guanylornithine, measures transfer and competing-reaction losses, and quantifies recoverable free arginine.\"\n}", "parsed_response": {"pathway_evaluations": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "reaction_scores": {"rxn_001": 0.88, "rxn_002": 0.92, "rxn_003": 0.88, "rxn_004": 0.91, "rxn_009": 0.87, "rxn_010": 0.88, "rxn_011": 0.84, "rxn_012": 0.81, "rxn_013": 0.88, "rxn_014": 0.88, "rxn_015": 0.8, "rxn_016": 0.75}, "chemical_feasibility": 0.83, "pathway_coherence": 0.87, "environmental_progression": 0.57, "target_achievement": 0.69, "overall_confidence": 0.74, "feedback": "This is the strongest supported route in the submitted network. Aldehyde-stage cyanamide trapping and subsequent homologation follow the experimentally supported Patel et al. (2015) chemistry through the arginine alpha-aminonitrile. Acidic nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis are chemically conventional, but selective recovery of free arginine from this particular reaction mixture is not demonstrated. Rapid interception of the photogenerated aldehyde must be established rather than assuming a stable, transferable aldehyde inventory. Surface photochemistry followed by acidic thermal processing is not intrinsically inconsistent, but numerical operating conditions and a physical transfer protocol are absent.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_015", "rxn_016"], "suggestions": ["Demonstrate rxn_015 and rxn_016 with authenticated arginine alpha-aminonitrile, measuring argi